/* TIH — Authored per-topic teaching notes for the IELTS Masterclass
   (Beginner to Band 9). The course player renders these directly (real
   textbook-style explanations: definition → key points/steps → worked
   example → why it matters) instead of the generic lesson template. Keyed
   by topic title (numbering/case ignored). Covers all 15 pathway modules. */
(function () {
  if (typeof window === 'undefined') return;
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES = window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES || {};
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES['ielts'] = {
  "English Alphabet & Pronunciation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 English Alphabet & Pronunciation</strong><br><span>Every strong English speaker starts with clear letters and sounds.</span></div><p>The English alphabet has 26 letters: five vowels (a, e, i, o, u) and 21 consonants. Each letter has a name and one or more sounds. Pronunciation means saying these sounds correctly so people understand you.</p><p>Many letters in English do not sound the way they look, so practice is important.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Vowels</strong> – a, e, i, o, u carry the main sound in most words.</li><li><strong>Consonants</strong> – the other 21 letters shape words around vowels.</li><li><strong>Letter name vs sound</strong> – the letter 'C' is named 'see' but can sound like 'k' in cat.</li><li><strong>Spelling out loud</strong> – useful when giving your name or address to an officer.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A clerk in Monrovia asks for your name. You say: 'S - A - M - U - E - L, Samuel.' Speaking each letter clearly helps them write it correctly on your form.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>In the IELTS Listening test you often hear names and words spelled out. Knowing letter sounds well lets you catch them the first time, and clear pronunciation builds confidence for the Speaking test.</p></div>",
  "Basic Grammar": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Basic Grammar</strong><br><span>Grammar is the set of rules that hold your sentences together.</span></div><p>Grammar tells us how to arrange words so a sentence makes sense. It covers verbs, tenses, subjects and agreement. Good grammar helps listeners and readers understand exactly what you mean.</p><p>You do not need perfect grammar to start, but small rules make a big difference.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Subject-verb agreement</strong> – 'She walks' not 'She walk'.</li><li><strong>Tenses</strong> – past (walked), present (walk), future (will walk) show time.</li><li><strong>Articles</strong> – use 'a', 'an', and 'the' before nouns.</li><li><strong>Word order</strong> – English usually follows subject + verb + object.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Instead of 'My brother go to market every day,' say 'My brother goes to the market every day.' The small changes make the sentence correct and clear.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Both IELTS Writing and Speaking are scored on grammar. Even simple, correct sentences earn marks, while grammar mistakes can confuse the examiner. Building basic grammar now creates a strong base for every skill.</p></div>",
  "Parts of Speech": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Parts of Speech</strong><br><span>Every word in English has a job to do.</span></div><p>Parts of speech are the different types of words we use to build sentences. The main ones are nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, pronouns, prepositions and conjunctions. Knowing them helps you understand how words work together.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Noun</strong> – a person, place or thing: teacher, Monrovia, book.</li><li><strong>Verb</strong> – an action or state: run, eat, is.</li><li><strong>Adjective</strong> – describes a noun: tall, happy, blue.</li><li><strong>Adverb</strong> – describes a verb: quickly, well, often.</li><li><strong>Pronoun</strong> – replaces a noun: he, she, it, they.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In 'The kind woman sells fresh fish quickly,' 'woman' and 'fish' are nouns, 'kind' and 'fresh' are adjectives, 'sells' is a verb, and 'quickly' is an adverb.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Understanding parts of speech helps you form correct sentences in IELTS Writing and choose the right words in Speaking. It also helps you follow the grammar in Reading passages more easily.</p></div>",
  "Common Vocabulary": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Common Vocabulary</strong><br><span>Words are the bricks you use to build every sentence.</span></div><p>Vocabulary means the words you know and can use. Common vocabulary covers everyday words about home, food, work, school and travel. The more useful words you know, the easier it is to speak, write and understand.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Everyday nouns</strong> – market, home, road, money, phone.</li><li><strong>Common verbs</strong> – go, buy, work, help, learn.</li><li><strong>Useful adjectives</strong> – big, small, cheap, busy, kind.</li><li><strong>Learn in groups</strong> – study words by topic to remember them faster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Talking about your day: 'In the morning I go to work by taxi. At lunch I buy rice and fish. In the evening I help my family at home.' These simple words describe a full day.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A wide vocabulary raises your score in every part of IELTS. In Speaking and Writing you can express ideas clearly, and in Listening and Reading you recognise more words, so you understand more of what you hear and read.</p></div>",
  "Sentence Structure": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Sentence Structure</strong><br><span>A clear sentence carries your idea from your mind to another person.</span></div><p>Sentence structure is the way words are arranged to make a complete thought. A basic English sentence has a subject (who or what) and a verb (the action), and often an object. Good structure makes your meaning clear.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Subject</strong> – who or what the sentence is about.</li><li><strong>Verb</strong> – the action or state.</li><li><strong>Object</strong> – who or what receives the action.</li><li><strong>Complete sentence</strong> – must express one full idea and start with a capital letter, end with a full stop.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">'My sister (subject) sells (verb) vegetables (object) at Waterside market.' This sentence is complete and easy to understand because the parts are in the right order.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>In IELTS Writing, well-structured sentences make your ideas clear and earn higher marks. In Speaking, they help you sound organised. Mastering basic structure now prepares you to build longer, more complex sentences later.</p></div>",
  "Daily Conversations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Daily Conversations</strong><br><span>Real English lives in the small talks we have every day.</span></div><p>Daily conversations are the simple exchanges we have with people around us: greeting a neighbour, asking a price, or talking about the weather. Practising these short, friendly talks builds your confidence to speak English naturally, without fear of making mistakes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Openers</strong> – 'How are you?', 'How is the family?'</li><li><strong>Everyday topics</strong> – food, weather, work, plans.</li><li><strong>Polite words</strong> – please, thank you, sorry, excuse me.</li><li><strong>Take turns</strong> – listen, then respond; conversation goes both ways.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">At the shop: 'Good morning. How much is the bread?' 'It is fifty dollars.' 'Okay, I will take two. Thank you.' A short, polite conversation like this happens many times each day.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The IELTS Speaking test feels like a friendly conversation. Practising daily talks makes you comfortable answering questions naturally and without long pauses, which helps you speak with confidence on test day.</p></div>",
  "Greetings & Introductions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Greetings & Introductions</strong><br><span>A warm hello opens the door to every conversation.</span></div><p>Greetings are the words we use to say hello, and introductions are how we tell people who we are. Doing this well makes a good first impression, whether meeting a teacher, an employer or a new friend.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Greetings</strong> – 'Good morning', 'Hello', 'Good evening'.</li><li><strong>Introducing yourself</strong> – 'My name is...', 'I am from...'</li><li><strong>Introducing others</strong> – 'This is my friend, Grace.'</li><li><strong>Responding</strong> – 'Nice to meet you', 'Pleased to meet you.'</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Meeting a new classmate: 'Hello, my name is Samuel. I am from Monrovia. Nice to meet you.' 'Nice to meet you too, Samuel. I am Grace.' A simple, friendly start to knowing someone.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The IELTS Speaking test begins with the examiner greeting you and asking about yourself. Confident greetings and introductions help you start strongly and set a calm, positive tone for the rest of the test.</p></div>",
  "Numbers, Dates & Time": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Numbers, Dates & Time</strong><br><span>Numbers, dates and time shape almost every plan we make.</span></div><p>This topic covers how to say and understand numbers, calendar dates, and the time of day in English. We use them to talk about prices, ages, appointments and schedules every single day.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Numbers</strong> – one, two, twenty, one hundred; and prices like 250.</li><li><strong>Dates</strong> – 'the 12th of August' or 'August 12th'.</li><li><strong>Time</strong> – 'half past seven', 'quarter to nine', 'eight o'clock'.</li><li><strong>Days and months</strong> – Monday to Sunday, January to December.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Booking a clinic visit: 'Your appointment is on Monday, the 12th of August, at half past nine in the morning.' Understanding each number and time detail helps you arrive correctly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Listening often tests numbers, dates and times in bookings, forms and phone messages. Catching them correctly earns easy marks. These skills also help in real life with jobs, travel and appointments.</p></div>",
  "Family & Friends": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Family & Friends</strong><br><span>The people closest to us are often the easiest to talk about.</span></div><p>This topic teaches the words we use to describe family members and friends, and how to talk about them. Family and friends are common, comfortable subjects in everyday English.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Family words</strong> – mother, father, brother, sister, aunt, uncle, cousin.</li><li><strong>Relationships</strong> – 'She is my older sister.'</li><li><strong>Friends</strong> – best friend, neighbour, classmate.</li><li><strong>Describing them</strong> – kind, funny, hardworking, helpful.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">'I live with my mother, my two brothers and my grandmother in Paynesville. My best friend, Grace, lives next door. She is very kind and we walk to school together.' This describes family and friends simply.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Family and friends are favourite topics in the IELTS Speaking test, especially in Part 1. Being ready to describe the people in your life helps you answer smoothly and with real, personal detail.</p></div>",
  "Describing People": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Describing People</strong><br><span>A good description helps others picture the person you mean.</span></div><p>Describing people means using words to talk about how someone looks and what kind of person they are. We describe both their appearance, meaning how they look, and their personality, meaning the kind of character they have inside.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Appearance</strong> – tall, short, slim, dark hair, kind face.</li><li><strong>Age</strong> – young, old, middle-aged, in her thirties.</li><li><strong>Personality</strong> – friendly, patient, hardworking, honest.</li><li><strong>Full sentences</strong> – 'He is tall and always smiling.'</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Describing a teacher: 'My teacher is a tall, middle-aged man with short grey hair. He is very patient and always helps students who do not understand.' Appearance and personality are combined clearly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>In IELTS Speaking Part 2 you may be asked to describe a person you admire. Knowing describing words lets you speak for the full two minutes with clear detail, which raises your fluency and vocabulary scores.</p></div>",
  "Listening for Names and Numbers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Listening for Names and Numbers</strong><br><span>Names and numbers carry the exact details you must catch.</span></div><p>This skill is about hearing names and numbers correctly, even when they come quickly. Names are often spelled out, and numbers include prices, phone numbers and ages. Careful listening captures each one.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the question first so you know what detail to expect.</li><li>Listen for spelling; write each letter as you hear it.</li><li>For numbers, write digits quickly, then check them.</li><li>Watch for corrections, like 'sorry, that is 7 not 8'.</li><li>Review your answer for correct spelling and full numbers.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">You hear: 'My name is Kollie, K-O-L-L-I-E, and my number is 0 - 7 - 7 - double 5 - 2.' You write 'Kollie' and '07755 2', catching the spelled letters and the word 'double' for two fives.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The IELTS Listening test Section 1 is full of names and numbers on forms and bookings. These are marks you can win easily with focused listening, and the skill also helps in real phone calls and appointments.</p></div>",
  "Listening for Directions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Listening for Directions</strong><br><span>Good listeners can find any place from spoken directions alone.</span></div><p>This skill helps you follow directions you hear, such as how to reach a place or find a room on a map. You listen for movement words and landmarks, then trace the route in your mind or on a map.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Find the starting point on the map before listening.</li><li>Listen for direction words: left, right, straight, past, next to.</li><li>Follow the route with your finger or pen as you hear it.</li><li>Note landmarks like 'opposite the market' or 'near the bridge'.</li><li>Stop where the speaker stops and mark your answer.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">You hear: 'From the school gate, walk straight, then turn left at the church. The clinic is on your right, opposite the market.' Following each step leads you exactly to the clinic.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Listening often uses maps where you label places from spoken directions. Tracking direction words carefully earns these marks. The skill also helps in daily life when someone tells you how to reach a place in town.</p></div>",
  "Everyday Conversations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Everyday Conversations</strong><br><span>Ordinary talk holds the details tests love to check.</span></div><p>Everyday conversations in listening are short talks between people about normal topics, such as shopping, plans or a problem to solve. You listen to understand who is speaking, what they want, and any details they agree on.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify the speakers and the situation quickly.</li><li>Listen for the main purpose of the conversation.</li><li>Catch key details like times, places and choices.</li><li>Notice when a speaker changes their mind.</li><li>Match what you heard to the answer options.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two friends plan a meeting: 'Let us meet at noon.' 'I cannot, I work until one.' 'Okay, two o'clock then.' The final agreed time is two o'clock, not noon, so you must follow the change.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Listening uses everyday conversations in the early sections. Learning to follow natural talk and catch changing details prepares you for these questions and builds understanding for real conversations at work and in the community.</p></div>",
  "Understanding Simple Instructions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Understanding Simple Instructions</strong><br><span>Following instructions well shows you truly understood.</span></div><p>This skill is about listening to instructions and doing exactly what is asked. Instructions tell you steps to follow, in order, such as how to fill a form or complete a task. Order and detail matter a lot.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Listen for signal words: first, then, next, after that, finally.</li><li>Note the order of steps carefully.</li><li>Catch specific details like which box to tick or where to write.</li><li>Do not skip a step, even a small one.</li><li>Check that your actions match every instruction given.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A teacher says: 'First write your name at the top. Then answer questions one to five. Do not answer question six.' Following this exactly means you skip question six, as instructed.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Listening and the test itself require following instructions, like 'write no more than two words'. Missing an instruction costs marks even when your English is good. This skill protects your score and helps in school and work tasks.</p></div>",
  "Note Completion Practice": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Note Completion Practice</strong><br><span>Notes capture the key words while the speaker keeps talking.</span></div><p>Note completion is a Listening task where you fill missing words in a set of short notes as you listen. The notes follow the order of the recording, and you write the exact words you hear, usually one to three words.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the notes first and guess what type of word fits each gap.</li><li>Underline the words around each gap to guide your listening.</li><li>Listen and write the exact word or words you hear.</li><li>Obey the word limit, such as 'no more than two words'.</li><li>Check spelling and grammar at the end.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The notes say: 'Meeting place: ______'. You hear: 'We will meet at the community hall in Paynesville.' You write 'community hall', staying within the two-word limit.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Note completion is common in IELTS Listening. Practising it trains you to predict answers and write quickly without falling behind the recording. These same habits help you take useful notes in class and at work.</p></div>",
  "Form Completion Practice": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Form Completion Practice</strong><br><span>Filling a form correctly is about catching each small detail.</span></div><p>Form completion is a Listening task where you complete a form, such as a registration or booking, with details you hear. You listen for names, numbers, addresses and choices, and write them in the right spaces.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the form to see what information each gap needs.</li><li>Predict the type of answer: a name, number or date.</li><li>Listen for the details and write them accurately.</li><li>Spell names correctly and write full numbers.</li><li>Follow the word limit and check your answers.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A form asks for 'Surname' and 'Phone'. You hear: 'The name is Tolbert, T-O-L-B-E-R-T, and the phone is 0886 123 456.' You write 'Tolbert' and the full phone number in the correct boxes.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Form completion appears in IELTS Listening Section 1 and rewards careful listening for details. It mirrors real tasks like registering for school or opening a phone account, so the skill is useful well beyond the test.</p></div>",
  "Skimming and Scanning": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Skimming and Scanning</strong><br><span>Two fast reading skills help you find answers without reading every word.</span></div><p>Skimming means reading quickly to get the general idea of a text. Scanning means searching quickly for specific information, like a name or date. Together they save time in reading tasks.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Skim first: read the title, headings and first lines to get the topic.</li><li>Understand what the passage is generally about.</li><li>Read the question to know the exact detail you need.</li><li>Scan by moving your eyes fast to find that keyword.</li><li>Read closely only around the words you found.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">To find when a Monrovia market opens, you do not read the whole notice. You scan for the word 'open' or a time, spot 'Opens at 7 a.m.', and you have your answer in seconds.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The IELTS Reading test is long and timed, so you cannot read every word slowly. Skimming and scanning let you find answers fast and finish on time. These skills also help with newspapers, notices and documents in daily life.</p></div>",
  "Finding Main Ideas": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Finding Main Ideas</strong><br><span>Every paragraph has one big idea holding it together.</span></div><p>The main idea is the most important point a writer is making. Around it are smaller details that support or explain it. Finding the main idea helps you understand a text quickly and correctly.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the first and last sentences of the paragraph.</li><li>Ask, 'What is this mostly about?'</li><li>Separate the big idea from the small examples.</li><li>Say the main idea in your own few words.</li><li>Check that the details support the idea you chose.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A paragraph describes fishing, farming and trading in a coastal town. The details are the jobs; the main idea is that 'people in the town earn a living in many different ways.'</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Reading asks questions like choosing headings or matching main ideas to paragraphs. Being able to find the main idea quickly earns these marks and helps you understand any article, report or letter you read.</p></div>",
  "Reading Advertisements": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Reading Advertisements</strong><br><span>Adverts pack important details into just a few words.</span></div><p>Advertisements are short texts that sell a product, service or job. They use small print, prices, dates and contact details. Reading them well means finding the key facts quickly and understanding the offer.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the heading to see what is being offered.</li><li>Scan for key facts: price, place, time and contact.</li><li>Look for conditions in small print, like 'offer ends soon'.</li><li>Compare offers if there is more than one.</li><li>Decide what the advert wants you to do next.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A shop advert in Monrovia reads: 'Phones for sale. From 3,000 LD. Free case with every phone. Call 0886 555 111. This week only.' The key facts are the price, the free case, the number and the time limit.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Reading Section 1 often uses adverts and notices to test scanning for detail. Practising them sharpens your speed and accuracy, and the skill helps you make smart choices when shopping or job hunting in real life.</p></div>",
  "Reading Emails and Messages": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Reading Emails and Messages</strong><br><span>Short messages still carry important requests and details.</span></div><p>Emails and messages are written notes people send to share news, make requests or give information. Reading them means understanding who sent it, why, and what they want you to do.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Check the sender and the subject line first.</li><li>Read the greeting to see who it is for.</li><li>Find the main purpose: a request, invitation or update.</li><li>Note any details like dates, places or actions needed.</li><li>Decide what reply or action the message expects.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An email says: 'Dear Samuel, our study group will meet on Saturday at 4 p.m. at the library. Please bring your notes. Reply if you can come.' The purpose is a meeting invite, and you are asked to reply and bring notes.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Reading and the General Training test use emails and letters to check understanding of purpose and detail. Reading them well earns marks and prepares you for school, work and everyday communication.</p></div>",
  "Reading Short Articles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Reading Short Articles</strong><br><span>A short article gives you facts and ideas in a few paragraphs.</span></div><p>Short articles are brief pieces of writing on one topic, like a news story or a fact page. Reading them means understanding the main idea and the key details, and how the paragraphs connect.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the title to predict the topic.</li><li>Skim the whole article for the general idea.</li><li>Read each paragraph and find its main point.</li><li>Notice how paragraphs link to build the whole idea.</li><li>Summarise the article in one or two sentences.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A short article about clean water in a Liberian village explains the problem, then a new well, then the result. Reading paragraph by paragraph, you see the story move from problem to solution to benefit.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Reading passages are longer articles, so practising short ones builds the habit of finding main ideas and details together. This grows your reading stamina and understanding, step by step, toward the full test.</p></div>",
  "Vocabulary Building": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Vocabulary Building</strong><br><span>Growing your word bank opens up every English skill.</span></div><p>Vocabulary building means learning new words and remembering them so you can use them. The more words you know, the more you understand when reading and listening, and the better you express yourself when speaking and writing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Choose a few new words each day from what you read.</li><li>Learn each word's meaning and an example sentence.</li><li>Group words by topic, such as health or work.</li><li>Use new words in your own speaking and writing soon.</li><li>Review old words often so you do not forget them.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">You read the word 'reliable'. You learn it means someone you can trust, and you make a sentence: 'My friend Grace is very reliable; she always keeps her promises.' Now the word is yours.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Vocabulary affects your score in all four IELTS skills. A strong word bank helps you understand Reading and Listening and lets you express ideas clearly in Writing and Speaking. Building words daily is one of the best ways to raise your band.</p></div>",
  "Sentence Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Sentence Writing</strong><br><span>Every good essay is built one clear sentence at a time.</span></div><p>A sentence is a group of words that shares one complete idea. In English, a full sentence needs a subject (who or what) and a verb (the action or state). If either part is missing, the reader feels lost. Learning to build strong, simple sentences is the first step to writing well in IELTS.</p><p>Start small and correct, then grow. It is better to write short sentences that are right than long ones that confuse.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Choose your subject: the person or thing you are writing about.</li><li>Add a verb that matches the subject in time and number.</li><li>Complete the idea with extra details if needed.</li><li>Check that it can stand alone and makes sense.</li><li>End with a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Weak: The market in Monrovia very busy. (no verb)<br>Strong: The market in Monrovia is very busy on Saturdays.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Writing is scored partly on grammar and clarity. Clear, complete sentences show the examiner you control the language, and they form the foundation for the paragraphs and essays you will write later.</p></div>",
  "Paragraph Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Paragraph Writing</strong><br><span>A paragraph groups related sentences around one main idea.</span></div><p>A paragraph is a set of sentences that work together to explain a single point. It usually begins with a topic sentence that tells the reader the main idea, followed by supporting sentences that give reasons, details, or examples. A short closing sentence can wrap up the thought.</p><p>Good paragraphs keep one idea per paragraph. When you move to a new idea, you start a new paragraph.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Write a topic sentence stating your main idea.</li><li>Add two or three supporting sentences with details or examples.</li><li>Use linking words such as because, for example, and also.</li><li>Finish with a sentence that closes the idea.</li><li>Check that every sentence connects to the topic sentence.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Topic: Farming is important in Bong County. Support: Many families grow rice and cassava to feed themselves and earn money. For example, my aunt sells vegetables at the local market every week. This work supports the whole community.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Writing Task 1 and Task 2 both need well-organised paragraphs. Clear structure earns marks for Coherence and Cohesion and makes your ideas easy for the examiner to follow.</p></div>",
  "Describing Places": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Describing Places</strong><br><span>Paint a picture with words so the reader can see the place too.</span></div><p>Describing a place means using clear details about what it looks like, where it is, and how it feels to be there. You use adjectives (busy, quiet, green), prepositions of place (near, beside, in the centre), and sense words to help the reader imagine it.</p><p>Organise your description in a logical order, such as from general to specific, or from outside to inside.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Name the place and say where it is located.</li><li>Describe its size and main features.</li><li>Add sensory details: sights, sounds, and atmosphere.</li><li>Explain why it is special or how it makes you feel.</li><li>Use varied adjectives instead of repeating nice or good.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Waterside Market in Monrovia is a large, lively place near the coast. Colourful stalls line the narrow paths, sellers call out prices, and the smell of grilled fish fills the air. It feels crowded but full of energy.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>IELTS Speaking Part 2 often asks you to describe a place you know, and Writing tasks may ask about locations. Rich, organised description shows a wide vocabulary and helps you speak or write for longer with confidence.</p></div>",
  "Writing Informal Emails": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writing Informal Emails</strong><br><span>Write to friends and family the friendly, natural way.</span></div><p>An informal email is a message you send to someone you know well, such as a friend, cousin, or classmate. The tone is warm and relaxed. You can use contractions (I am becomes I'm), simple greetings, and everyday language, but your spelling and grammar should still be correct.</p><p>Informal emails follow a simple shape: greeting, opening, main message, and a friendly closing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Start with a friendly greeting such as Hi Musu or Dear James.</li><li>Open with a warm line like How are you? or Hope you are well.</li><li>Write your main message clearly in short paragraphs.</li><li>Close with a phrase like Take care or Write back soon.</li><li>Sign off with your name.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Hi Kollie, How are you? I am writing to invite you to my sister's wedding in Gbarnga next month. It will be a big celebration and we would love for you to come. Let me know if you can make it. Take care, Samuel</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>General Training Writing Task 1 often asks for a letter or email, sometimes informal. Knowing the right tone and structure helps you match the task and earn strong marks for task achievement.</p></div>",
  "Common Grammar Errors": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Common Grammar Errors</strong><br><span>Fixing small mistakes can quickly raise your band score.</span></div><p>Grammar errors are small language mistakes that make writing harder to understand. Many learners repeat the same few errors, so once you learn to spot them, you can fix them fast. Common ones include subject-verb agreement, wrong verb tense, missing articles, and plural mistakes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Subject-verb agreement</strong> – He go should be He goes.</li><li><strong>Articles</strong> – Use a, an, and the correctly: I saw a dog, then the dog ran.</li><li><strong>Plurals</strong> – Many student should be many students.</li><li><strong>Verb tense</strong> – Keep your time frame steady: Yesterday I went, not Yesterday I go.</li><li><strong>Prepositions</strong> – Say arrive in Monrovia, not arrive to Monrovia.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Error: My brother work in a school and he have two childrens.<br>Correct: My brother works in a school and he has two children.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Grammatical Range and Accuracy is one quarter of your Writing and Speaking scores. Reducing repeated errors makes your English clearer and pushes your band higher with steady practice.</p></div>",
  "Self-Introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Self-Introduction</strong><br><span>A confident hello sets the tone for your whole speaking test.</span></div><p>A self-introduction is a short, clear way of telling someone who you are. In IELTS Speaking Part 1, the examiner often begins by asking your name, where you are from, and about your daily life. Answering smoothly helps you relax and start well.</p><p>Keep it natural and friendly, as if meeting a new person for the first time.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Greet politely and state your name clearly.</li><li>Say where you are from or where you live.</li><li>Add one detail about your work or studies.</li><li>Mention one interest to sound natural.</li><li>Speak slowly, smile, and make eye contact.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Good morning. My name is Samuel Tolbert and I am from Monrovia, Liberia. I work at a small tech centre where I help young people learn computer skills. In my free time, I enjoy reading and playing football.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The first minute of IELTS Speaking shapes your confidence and the examiner's first impression. A clear self-introduction helps you settle your nerves and speak fluently for the rest of the test.</p></div>",
  "Talking About Family": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Talking About Family</strong><br><span>Family is a warm, familiar topic that helps you speak easily.</span></div><p>Talking about family means describing the people you live with or are related to, such as parents, siblings, and relatives. In IELTS Speaking, family questions are common because everyone can talk about them. You describe who they are, what they do, and how you feel about them.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Family words</strong> – parents, siblings, relatives, uncle, aunt, grandparents.</li><li><strong>Describing people</strong> – kind, hardworking, funny, caring.</li><li><strong>Relationships</strong> – close to, get along with, look up to.</li><li><strong>Extend answers</strong> – add reasons and examples, not just yes or no.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">I come from a close family in Kakata. My father is a farmer and my mother sells goods at the market. I have two younger sisters who I look after. We spend Sundays together after church, and I really enjoy that time because we all share stories.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Family questions appear often in Speaking Part 1 and sometimes Part 2. Being ready with vocabulary and examples lets you speak longer and more naturally, which boosts your fluency score.</p></div>",
  "Talking About Hobbies": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Talking About Hobbies</strong><br><span>Sharing what you love to do makes speaking feel easy and fun.</span></div><p>A hobby is an activity you enjoy doing in your free time, such as playing football, cooking, singing, or reading. When you talk about hobbies in IELTS, you explain what you do, when you do it, and why you like it. Because you enjoy the topic, it is easier to speak with energy.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Naming hobbies</strong> – I enjoy, I am keen on, I love, I am into.</li><li><strong>Frequency words</strong> – every day, on weekends, whenever I can.</li><li><strong>Reasons</strong> – It helps me relax, It keeps me fit, It is exciting.</li><li><strong>Details</strong> – say where, who with, and how long you have done it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">One of my favourite hobbies is playing football. I play with my friends at the local field in Paynesville every Saturday. I have loved the game since I was a boy because it keeps me healthy and it is a great way to spend time with friends.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Hobbies are a favourite IELTS Speaking topic in Parts 1 and 2. Talking about something you enjoy helps you use natural language and speak fluently, which the examiner rewards.</p></div>",
  "Talking About School": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Talking About School</strong><br><span>Your school days give you plenty to talk about with confidence.</span></div><p>Talking about school means describing where you studied, the subjects you took, your teachers, and your memories. IELTS often asks about education because it connects to everyone's life. You can talk about the past (your school days) or the present (your current studies).</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>School words</strong> – primary school, secondary school, subjects, teachers, classmates.</li><li><strong>Subjects</strong> – mathematics, English, science, geography.</li><li><strong>Opinions</strong> – My favourite subject was, I found it difficult, I enjoyed.</li><li><strong>Past tense</strong> – I studied, I learned, my teacher taught us.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">I went to a secondary school in Buchanan. My favourite subject was English because my teacher made every lesson interesting with stories and games. I found mathematics harder, but my classmates helped me practise, and I slowly improved over the years.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Education questions appear in Speaking Part 1 and as Part 2 cue cards. Knowing school vocabulary and using the correct past tense shows range and accuracy, helping you reach a higher band.</p></div>",
  "Talking About Work": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Talking About Work</strong><br><span>Describing your job or dream job is a common speaking task.</span></div><p>Talking about work means explaining what you do, where you do it, and how you feel about it. If you are not working yet, you can describe the job you hope to have. IELTS examiners ask about work to see how well you describe daily routines and responsibilities.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Job words</strong> – employer, colleague, salary, responsibilities, workplace.</li><li><strong>Describing duties</strong> – I am responsible for, my job involves, I deal with.</li><li><strong>Opinions</strong> – I find it rewarding, It can be stressful, I enjoy.</li><li><strong>Future plans</strong> – I would like to, I hope to, my goal is.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">I work at a community tech hub in Monrovia. My job involves teaching young people basic computer skills and helping them find online opportunities. I find it very rewarding because I can see students grow, although preparing lessons can sometimes be tiring.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Work is one of the most common Speaking Part 1 topics. Being able to describe your role clearly, with reasons and feelings, helps you extend answers and demonstrate strong vocabulary and grammar.</p></div>",
  "Basic Fluency Practice": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Basic Fluency Practice</strong><br><span>Fluency means speaking smoothly, not perfectly.</span></div><p>Fluency is the ability to keep talking at a steady pace without too many long pauses. It does not mean speaking fast or never making mistakes. In IELTS Speaking, examiners want to hear you express ideas naturally and connect your sentences. Regular practice builds this skill.</p><p>The secret is to keep going even when you make a small error, just as you do in your own language.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Speak English aloud every day, even for five minutes.</li><li>Use filler phrases like Well, Let me think, and Actually to fill pauses.</li><li>Link ideas with words such as and, but, because, and so.</li><li>Do not stop to fix small mistakes; keep the flow going.</li><li>Record yourself and listen to track your progress.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Practice answer: Well, let me think. On weekends I usually visit my grandmother in Careysburg because I enjoy her cooking, and afterwards I meet my friends to play football. So my weekends are quite busy but also very relaxing.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Fluency and Coherence is one quarter of your Speaking score. Practising daily helps you speak with less hesitation, giving the examiner the impression of a confident, natural speaker.</p></div>",
  "Multiple Choice Questions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Multiple Choice Questions</strong><br><span>Choose the right answer by listening for meaning, not just words.</span></div><p>In IELTS Listening, multiple choice questions give you a question and several options (usually A, B, or C). You listen to the recording and pick the correct one. The tricky part is that the audio often uses different words from the options, so you must understand the meaning, not just match words.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the question and all options before the audio starts.</li><li>Underline key words in the question.</li><li>Listen for the idea, expecting paraphrases and synonyms.</li><li>Watch out for distractors that mention wrong options first.</li><li>Choose the best answer and move on quickly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Question: Why was the trip cancelled? A) bad weather B) low cost C) few people. Audio: We had to call it off because not enough students signed up. Answer: C, because few people is a paraphrase of not enough students.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Multiple choice appears in many Listening sections and can feel tricky because of distractors. Learning to listen for meaning rather than exact words helps you avoid traps and score more marks.</p></div>",
  "Map Labelling": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Map Labelling</strong><br><span>Follow directions on a map with your ears, not just your eyes.</span></div><p>Map labelling asks you to write the correct name or letter on a map or plan as you listen. The speaker describes locations using direction words. Your job is to follow along and place each label correctly. Understanding direction and position words is the key skill.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Study the map and note the starting point or entrance.</li><li>Learn direction words: left, right, opposite, next to, behind, at the corner.</li><li>Listen and trace the route with your finger or pen.</li><li>Write each answer as soon as you hear it.</li><li>Do not fall behind; keep moving with the speaker.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Audio: From the main gate, walk straight ahead. The library is on your right, just past the canteen. Action: Find the gate, move forward, and label the building on the right after the canteen as the library.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Map tasks appear in Listening Section 2, often about places like a college or town. Mastering direction vocabulary lets you follow the speaker easily and pick up quick, reliable marks.</p></div>",
  "Table Completion": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Table Completion</strong><br><span>Fill the gaps in a table by listening for the right details.</span></div><p>Table completion gives you a table with some information filled in and some blank spaces. As you listen, you write the missing words or numbers in the correct boxes. The rows and columns guide you, so you always know what type of answer to expect, such as a price, a date, or a name.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the table headings to understand what each column means.</li><li>Predict what kind of word fits each gap: number, name, or time.</li><li>Follow the order, as answers usually come in sequence.</li><li>Obey the word limit, such as no more than two words.</li><li>Check spelling and copy numbers exactly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Table: Tour | Day | Price. Gap under Price for the Saturday tour. Audio: The Saturday tour costs fifteen dollars per person. Answer: 15 dollars, written in the Price box for Saturday.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Table completion is common in Listening Sections 1 and 3. Because the layout tells you what to expect, careful prediction and accurate spelling turn these into dependable points toward your band score.</p></div>",
  "Sentence Completion": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Sentence Completion</strong><br><span>Complete each sentence with the exact words you hear.</span></div><p>In sentence completion, you see sentences with missing words and fill the gaps using words from the recording. You must keep to the word limit, for example no more than two words or a number. The sentence grammar gives you strong clues about what type of word is missing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read each sentence and find the gap.</li><li>Decide the word type needed: noun, verb, adjective, or number.</li><li>Listen for the exact word, watching for paraphrase around it.</li><li>Write only the missing words, not the whole sentence.</li><li>Stay within the word limit and check spelling.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Sentence: The new clinic will open in ______. Audio: We expect to open the clinic in December this year. Answer: December (one word, staying within the limit).</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Sentence completion tests both listening and grammar awareness. Predicting the word type before you listen helps you catch the answer quickly and avoid losing marks to spelling or word-limit mistakes.</p></div>",
  "Summary Completion": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Summary Completion</strong><br><span>Fill the blanks in a short summary as you follow the recording.</span></div><p>Summary completion gives you a short paragraph that summarises part of the recording, with several gaps to fill. Sometimes you choose words from a box, and sometimes you write words you hear. The summary follows the order of the audio, so you can track your place as you listen.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the whole summary first to understand the topic.</li><li>Predict the type of word each gap needs.</li><li>If a word box is given, read the options carefully.</li><li>Listen in order and fill gaps as you go.</li><li>Check grammar so each completed sentence reads correctly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Summary: The project helped farmers grow more ______ during the dry season. Audio: Thanks to the new wells, farmers produced far more vegetables even in the dry months. Answer: vegetables.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Summary completion appears in later Listening sections, which are often harder. Reading ahead and predicting word types keeps you on track with the speaker and helps you secure marks in the tougher parts of the test.</p></div>",
  "Academic Listening": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Academic Listening</strong><br><span>Follow lectures and discussions on academic topics with confidence.</span></div><p>Academic listening is the skill of understanding talks, lectures, and discussions like those in Listening Sections 3 and 4. The language is more formal, the topics are educational, and speakers may explain ideas, give opinions, or compare views. You need to follow the main ideas and catch important details.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the questions first to know what to listen for.</li><li>Listen for signposting words like firstly, however, and in conclusion.</li><li>Focus on main ideas before small details.</li><li>Note how speakers agree, disagree, or change their minds.</li><li>Keep listening even if you miss one answer; do not panic.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In a lecture on farming, the speaker says: Firstly, we will look at soil, and later at rainfall. The word firstly signals the first main point, helping you organise the information as you listen.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Sections 3 and 4 carry difficult questions and often decide higher band scores. Building academic listening skills, such as following signposts and main ideas, prepares you for both the test and real study abroad.</p></div>",
  "Matching Headings": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Matching Headings</strong><br><span>Find the main idea of each paragraph, not just a matching word.</span></div><p>In this task you are given a list of headings (usually labelled i, ii, iii...) and must match one heading to each paragraph or section of the passage. There are always more headings than paragraphs, so some headings are never used. This tests whether you can identify the central idea of a chunk of text rather than a single detail.</p><p>The trap is choosing a heading just because it repeats a word you saw. A paragraph can mention Monrovia three times yet be about something else entirely.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the whole paragraph quickly for the general point, not every word.</li><li>Ask yourself: in one sentence, what is this paragraph mainly saying?</li><li>Scan the heading list for the option that captures that main idea.</li><li>Watch for topic sentences, usually the first or last line.</li><li>Cross out headings as you use them and leave hardest paragraphs for last.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A paragraph describes how rice farmers in Lofa switched crops after floods, earned more, and inspired neighbours. Heading iv (The wider effects of one small change) fits better than heading ii (Problems caused by flooding), which only names a detail.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Matching Headings appears often in Academic Reading and rewards true comprehension. Mastering main ideas here also speeds up every other reading task and lifts your overall band.</p></div>",
  "True/False/Not Given": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 True/False/Not Given</strong><br><span>Judge statements against facts in the text, never your own knowledge.</span></div><p>You are given statements about factual information in the passage. Mark TRUE if the statement agrees with the text, FALSE if it contradicts the text, and NOT GIVEN if the text simply does not tell you. This is about facts stated by the writer, not opinions.</p><p>The hardest choice is between FALSE and NOT GIVEN. FALSE means the passage says the opposite; NOT GIVEN means the passage is silent on that point.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the statement and underline key words, especially numbers, names and words like all, always, only.</li><li>Find the matching part of the passage in order; answers follow the text order.</li><li>Compare meaning carefully, not just words.</li><li>If the text confirms it, mark TRUE; if it clashes, mark FALSE.</li><li>If you cannot find the information anywhere, mark NOT GIVEN and move on.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Text: The market in Ganta opens every weekday. Statement: The market opens on Saturdays. This is FALSE (weekday contradicts Saturday). But Statement: The market is the largest in the county is NOT GIVEN, because size is never mentioned.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>This question type catches learners who guess from experience. Training yourself to answer only from the text builds the precise reading discipline examiners reward across the whole test.</p></div>",
  "Yes/No/Not Given": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Yes/No/Not Given</strong><br><span>Same skill as True/False, but now you track the writer's opinions.</span></div><p>This task looks like True/False/Not Given, but here the statements are about the writer's views, claims or beliefs, not hard facts. Mark YES if the statement agrees with the writer's opinion, NO if it contradicts the writer, and NOT GIVEN if the writer never expresses that view.</p><p>Look for opinion language in the passage: words like believe, argue, suggest, unfortunately, clearly, should. These signal the author taking a position.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Underline the opinion in each statement, especially judgement words.</li><li>Locate the writer's view in the passage; answers stay in text order.</li><li>Decide whether the writer agrees (YES) or disagrees (NO) with the statement.</li><li>If the writer never gives that opinion, choose NOT GIVEN.</li><li>Do not confuse someone quoted in the text with the writer's own view.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Writer: Solar power is, without doubt, the smartest investment for rural Liberia. Statement: The writer thinks solar power is a wise choice. Answer: YES. Statement: The writer prefers solar over hydro. Answer: NOT GIVEN, since hydro is never compared.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Distinguishing opinion from fact is a core academic skill. It also strengthens your Writing and Speaking, where you must recognise and respond to a writer or speaker position.</p></div>",
  "Matching Information": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Matching Information</strong><br><span>Hunt for where a specific piece of information is hidden in the text.</span></div><p>In Matching Information you are given statements (for example, a description, a reason, or an example) and must decide which paragraph contains each one. Paragraphs are labelled A, B, C and so on. Unlike Matching Headings, you are not finding the main idea; you are finding a specific detail that could sit anywhere in a paragraph.</p><p>Any paragraph can be used once, more than once, or not at all, which makes guessing risky.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the statement and note exactly what type of information it describes.</li><li>Do not read in order; the answers jump around the passage.</li><li>Scan each paragraph for the specific detail, example or reason named.</li><li>Match on meaning; the words are usually paraphrased.</li><li>Do the easy, clearly located statements first, then return to the tricky ones.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Statement: A description of how a traditional method was replaced by a new one. You scan and find paragraph D explains how hand-pounding of cassava gave way to a small diesel mill. Paragraph D is the answer, even though the word replaced never appears.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>This task is one of the more time-consuming types, so practising fast scanning here protects your time for the rest of the Reading test and lifts your accuracy.</p></div>",
  "Diagram Labelling": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Diagram Labelling</strong><br><span>Match words from the text to the right parts of a picture.</span></div><p>Here the passage is paired with a diagram, such as a machine, a plant, a building, or a natural process. You must label parts of the diagram using words taken from the text, usually within a stated word limit (for example, NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS). The information you need is almost always in one section of the passage that describes the diagram.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Study the diagram first and understand what it shows overall.</li><li>Find the part of the passage that describes it, often signalled by position words.</li><li>Follow the description in order; texts usually move top to bottom or left to right.</li><li>Copy the exact words from the passage, spelled correctly.</li><li>Respect the word limit; going over means the answer is marked wrong.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A diagram shows a simple water filter. The text says: water enters the top layer of gravel, then passes through fine sand before collecting in the base. You label the top box gravel, the middle box sand, and the bottom box base, all copied straight from the passage.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Diagram Labelling rewards careful, orderly reading and correct spelling. These are quick, winnable marks that boost your Reading band when handled calmly.</p></div>",
  "Reading Academic Texts": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Reading Academic Texts</strong><br><span>Long, formal passages become manageable with the right reading strategy.</span></div><p>The Academic Reading test uses three long passages from books, journals and newspapers on topics like science, history and society. The language is formal and the vocabulary is wide. You are not expected to understand every word; you are expected to find answers efficiently within 60 minutes for 40 questions across all three passages.</p><p>The key skills are skimming for the general idea and scanning for specific details, then reading closely only where an answer lives.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Skim the passage in about two minutes to grasp the topic and structure.</li><li>Read the questions and underline key words before hunting for answers.</li><li>Scan for names, dates and keywords to jump to the right lines.</li><li>Read that section slowly to confirm the answer through meaning.</li><li>Manage time: about 20 minutes per passage, and never leave blanks.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Faced with a 900-word passage on rubber production, you skim to learn it covers history, method and economics. A question about employment sends you scanning for the word workers, where you read closely and find the answer in one sentence.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Speed and strategy, not perfect understanding, decide your Reading band. Building these habits early means you finish on time and answer with confidence.</p></div>",
  "Introduction to Task 1": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Introduction to Task 1</strong><br><span>Describe visual information clearly and objectively in 20 minutes.</span></div><p>In Academic Writing Task 1 you are shown a visual, such as a graph, chart, table, map or process, and asked to describe its main features in at least 150 words. You have about 20 minutes. You do not give opinions or reasons; you report what the data shows, selecting and comparing the most important information.</p><p>A strong answer has four parts: an introduction that paraphrases the question, an overview of the biggest trends, and two body paragraphs with specific detail.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the task and identify what the visual shows and the time period.</li><li>Write one introduction sentence in your own words.</li><li>Write an overview naming the two or three biggest features, no numbers needed.</li><li>Write two body paragraphs grouping and supporting details with data.</li><li>Check you have at least 150 words and no opinions.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The chart shows electricity access in three Liberian counties from 2000 to 2020 becomes: The bar chart illustrates the percentage of households with electricity in three counties over a twenty-year period.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Task 1 is 33 percent of your Writing score. A reliable four-part structure lets you write a full, focused answer every time, whatever visual appears.</p></div>",
  "Charts and Graphs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Charts and Graphs</strong><br><span>Learn to read any chart before you write a single word.</span></div><p>Charts and graphs present numbers visually so trends and comparisons are easy to see. In Task 1 you may meet line graphs, bar charts, pie charts and tables. Whatever the type, the job is the same: understand the axes or categories, spot the biggest changes and comparisons, and report them in clear, accurate English.</p><p>Always check the units and the time frame first, because misreading them can spoil the whole answer.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the title, axis labels and units before anything else.</li><li>Note whether the data changes over time or compares categories.</li><li>Find the highest and lowest points and any big changes.</li><li>Group similar items so your description is organised, not random.</li><li>Choose accurate data words: rise, fall, peak, the majority, roughly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graph tracks mobile phone users in West Africa from 2005 to 2020. You note the vertical axis is millions of users, the line climbs steadily, and it peaks in 2020. You then write: The number of mobile users rose sharply, reaching a peak of 40 million in 2020.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every Task 1 answer starts with reading the visual correctly. Building this habit prevents factual errors, which examiners penalise heavily, and sets up an accurate, high-band response.</p></div>",
  "Tables": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Tables</strong><br><span>Rows and columns hold many numbers, so you must select wisely.</span></div><p>A table presents data in rows and columns without any picture of the trend, so you have to find the patterns yourself. In Task 1 you should never describe every single number. Instead, select the highest and lowest figures, the biggest differences, and any clear trends, then group them into an organised description.</p><p>Reading across rows and down columns helps you spot which category leads and which lags.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the row and column headings and note the units.</li><li>Scan for the largest and smallest values in the whole table.</li><li>Look for patterns: which category is consistently high or low.</li><li>Choose the key figures to report; leave minor numbers out.</li><li>Group related data into two clear body paragraphs.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A table shows crop yields in four counties. You spot that Lofa has the highest rice yield every year while Grand Bassa has the lowest, and you write: Throughout the period, Lofa recorded the greatest rice output, whereas Grand Bassa consistently produced the least.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Tables test your ability to select and compare rather than list. Showing the examiner you can pick out significant figures is exactly what lifts your score above band 5.</p></div>",
  "Pie Charts": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Pie Charts</strong><br><span>Circles of slices show proportions, so think in percentages.</span></div><p>A pie chart divides a whole into slices that show proportions, usually as percentages that add up to 100. In Task 1 you often compare two or more pie charts, for example the same categories in two different years. Your task is to describe the largest and smallest slices and how proportions change between charts.</p><p>Use language of proportion: the majority, nearly half, a small fraction, one quarter.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the chart title and check what the whole represents.</li><li>Identify the biggest and smallest slices in each chart.</li><li>If there are two charts, compare how each slice changes.</li><li>Group similar-sized slices together in your description.</li><li>Use proportion phrases and accurate percentages.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two pie charts show household fuel use in a Liberian village in 2000 and 2020. Firewood falls from 70 percent to 45 percent while gas rises. You write: In 2000, firewood made up the majority of fuel use at 70 percent, but by 2020 this had dropped to under half.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Pie charts reward precise proportion vocabulary. Mastering phrases like the majority and a small proportion shows range and accuracy, both of which raise your Task 1 band.</p></div>",
  "Line Graphs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Line Graphs</strong><br><span>Lines that move over time are all about trends: up, down or flat.</span></div><p>A line graph shows how one or more things change over a period of time. The horizontal axis is usually time and the vertical axis is the measured quantity. Your main job is to describe the trends, meaning the direction of movement, and to mention key points such as peaks, low points and moments where lines cross.</p><p>Rich trend vocabulary is what separates a band 6 answer from a band 7 here.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Check both axes, the units and the time period.</li><li>Describe the overall trend of each line: rising, falling or stable.</li><li>Mark peaks, troughs and any point where lines meet or cross.</li><li>Use varied verbs and adverbs: increased sharply, fell gradually, levelled off.</li><li>Add specific figures with years to support each statement.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A line graph shows rainfall in Monrovia from January to December. You write: Rainfall rose steadily from January, peaked at 600 mm in July, then declined gradually to a low of 30 mm in December.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Line graphs are the most common Task 1 visual. Confident trend language and accurate data references let you write a full, high-scoring answer quickly and calmly.</p></div>",
  "Bar Charts": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Bar Charts</strong><br><span>Bars are made for comparing, so compare loudly and clearly.</span></div><p>A bar chart uses bars of different heights or lengths to compare categories. Sometimes it also shows change over time. Your task is to compare the categories, pointing out which are highest and lowest and by how much, and to note any clear patterns or changes if a time period is involved.</p><p>Comparison and superlative language is central: higher than, twice as many, the highest, far fewer.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the title and axis labels and note the units.</li><li>Decide whether the chart compares categories, tracks time, or both.</li><li>Identify the tallest and shortest bars.</li><li>Compare bars using comparatives and superlatives.</li><li>Group similar bars together for an organised description.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A bar chart compares students enrolled in five subjects at a Monrovia college. You write: Business attracted by far the most students at 300, roughly three times the number studying agriculture, which was the least popular subject with only 100.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Bar charts appear frequently and test comparison skills directly. Using accurate comparative language shows the range and precision that push your Writing score higher.</p></div>",
  "Process Diagrams": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Process Diagrams</strong><br><span>A series of stages to describe in order, from start to finish.</span></div><p>A process diagram shows how something is made or how a natural cycle works, presented as a series of stages linked by arrows. Unlike graphs, there are no numbers to compare. Instead you describe each step in sequence, using the passive voice and sequencing words to move the reader smoothly from beginning to end.</p><p>Count the stages first so your overview can state how many steps the process has.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify where the process begins and ends and count the stages.</li><li>Write an overview stating the number of steps and the general nature of the process.</li><li>Describe each stage in order, following the arrows.</li><li>Use sequencing words: first, next, after that, finally.</li><li>Use the passive voice, for example the beans are dried.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A diagram shows how cocoa is processed. You write: First, the pods are harvested. The beans are then removed and left to ferment. After that, they are dried in the sun before finally being roasted and packed for export.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Processes reward clear sequencing and correct passive forms. Practising them gives you an easy, structured answer when a process appears, so you are never caught off guard.</p></div>",
  "Maps": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Maps</strong><br><span>Two maps of the same place, and your job is to describe the changes.</span></div><p>Map tasks usually show the same location at two different times, for example a town in 1990 and today, and ask you to describe how it changed. There are no numbers. You describe what was added, removed, replaced or expanded, using location language and the past and present tenses to contrast the two maps.</p><p>Compass directions and position words are essential: to the north, in the south-east, beside the river.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Compare the two maps and note the main differences.</li><li>Write an overview describing the overall change, such as more built-up or greener.</li><li>Describe changes by area, using compass directions and position words.</li><li>Use change verbs: was built, was replaced by, was demolished, expanded.</li><li>Use past tense for the old map and present or present perfect for the new one.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two maps show a coastal village near Buchanan before and after development. You write: The farmland to the north was replaced by a hotel, while a new road was built along the coast, and the small jetty was expanded into a harbour.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Maps test location vocabulary and tense control. Preparing for them means the less common Task 1 types cannot surprise you on exam day.</p></div>",
  "Comparing Data": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Comparing Data</strong><br><span>Task 1 is mostly comparison, so the right language earns the marks.</span></div><p>Almost every Task 1 requires you to compare and contrast, whether across categories, groups or time. Comparing data well means choosing accurate comparative and superlative structures and varying them so your writing does not repeat the same phrase. This is a major part of the grammar and vocabulary examiners assess.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Comparatives: higher than, lower than, greater than, more slowly than.</li><li>Superlatives: the highest, the lowest, the most popular, the least common.</li><li>Multiples and fractions: twice as many, half as much, three times higher.</li><li>Similarity: the same as, similar to, roughly equal to.</li><li>Contrast linkers: whereas, while, in contrast, compared with.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Instead of writing Rice was 200 and cassava was 100, compare: Rice production was twice as high as cassava, whereas plantain output remained the lowest of the three crops throughout the period.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Comparison language directly affects your grammatical range and vocabulary scores. Learners who only list numbers stay at band 5; those who compare with variety reach band 7 and beyond.</p></div>",
  "Task 1 Vocabulary": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Task 1 Vocabulary</strong><br><span>The right words for trends, proportions and change lift your band fast.</span></div><p>Task 1 uses a special set of vocabulary for describing data. Having a ready bank of accurate words for movement, degree, proportion and time lets you write quickly and show the range examiners reward. The secret is variety: avoid repeating the same word, and pair verbs with matching adverbs.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Upward verbs: rise, increase, climb, grow, surge.</li><li>Downward verbs: fall, decrease, decline, drop, plunge.</li><li>Degree adverbs: sharply, steadily, gradually, slightly, dramatically.</li><li>Stability and peaks: remained stable, levelled off, peaked, reached a low.</li><li>Proportion: the majority, a significant proportion, a small fraction, roughly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Rather than writing went up three times, vary it: Enrolment rose sharply in 2010, increased gradually until 2015, then climbed steadily to a peak of 500 students in 2020.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A strong Task 1 vocabulary bank means you never freeze searching for words. Precise, varied language directly raises your lexical resource score, one of the four marking criteria.</p></div>",
  "Essay Structure": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Essay Structure</strong><br><span>A clear structure is the frame that holds every band-7 essay together.</span></div><p>In IELTS Writing Task 2 you write a short essay of at least 250 words in about 40 minutes. A strong structure has four parts: an introduction, two or three body paragraphs, and a conclusion. Each body paragraph carries one main idea, explained and supported with an example.</p><p>Structure is not decoration. Examiners reward essays that are easy to follow from the first line to the last.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the question twice and underline the task words.</li><li>Plan for two minutes: decide your position and two main ideas.</li><li>Write an introduction that paraphrases the question and states your view.</li><li>Give each main idea its own paragraph, with a reason and an example.</li><li>Finish with a short conclusion that restates your position.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Introduction model sentence: This essay will argue that free public transport in cities such as Monrovia would reduce traffic and help low-income workers most.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A quarter of your Task 2 score comes from Coherence and Cohesion. A predictable, well-organised structure lets the examiner see your ideas clearly and frees your mind to focus on grammar and vocabulary.</p></div>",
  "Opinion Essays": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Opinion Essays</strong><br><span>When the question asks what you think, take a clear side and defend it.</span></div><p>Opinion essays often begin with phrases like To what extent do you agree or disagree, or Do you think the advantages outweigh the disadvantages. Your job is to choose one position and support it strongly throughout the essay. Sitting on the fence usually lowers your score.</p><p>You can fully agree, fully disagree, or partly agree, but your view must stay consistent from introduction to conclusion.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Decide your opinion before you write anything.</li><li>State that opinion clearly in the introduction.</li><li>Devote each body paragraph to one reason that supports your view.</li><li>Add a real or realistic example for each reason.</li><li>Restate your opinion in different words in the conclusion.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Sample thesis: I strongly agree that young people should learn a practical trade, because skills such as tailoring or solar installation create income even where office jobs are scarce.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Examiners look for a clear position that is developed and never contradicted. A firm, consistent opinion shows control of Task Response and makes your whole essay feel purposeful and confident.</p></div>",
  "Discussion Essays": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Discussion Essays</strong><br><span>Show both sides fairly, then tell the examiner where you stand.</span></div><p>Discussion essays usually say Discuss both views and give your own opinion. This means you must present two opposing sides honestly and then add your personal view. Many candidates forget the opinion part and lose marks, so read the question carefully.</p><p>Balance is key: give each view a full paragraph before you commit to your own position.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Introduce the topic and mention that opinions differ.</li><li>State your own opinion briefly at the end of the introduction.</li><li>Write one paragraph explaining the first view with an example.</li><li>Write a second paragraph explaining the opposing view.</li><li>Conclude by restating which view you find more convincing and why.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Model sentence: Some people believe children should study only academic subjects, while others feel farming and craft skills are equally valuable; in my view, a balance of both prepares young Liberians best.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The instruction has two clear tasks, discuss both views and give your opinion. Completing both fully is essential for a strong Task Response score. Ignoring either half caps your band no matter how good your English is.</p></div>",
  "Problem-Solution Essays": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Problem-Solution Essays</strong><br><span>Name the causes, then offer realistic answers that actually fit the problem.</span></div><p>These questions describe an issue and ask you to explain its causes or problems and suggest solutions. A common form is: What problems does this cause and how can they be solved. Your essay must clearly separate the problems from the solutions so the reader never gets confused.</p><p>Keep solutions linked to the problems you raised, not random ideas.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Paraphrase the problem in your introduction.</li><li>Dedicate one body paragraph to the main causes or problems.</li><li>Dedicate a second body paragraph to matching solutions.</li><li>Support each solution with an example or expected result.</li><li>Summarise the problem and the most effective solution in the conclusion.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Model sentence: Frequent power cuts damage small businesses; one practical solution is to expand affordable solar systems, which many households in West Africa already use successfully.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Examiners check that every solution answers a stated problem. Clear cause-and-solution links show logical thinking and boost both Task Response and Coherence, two of the four scoring areas in Task 2.</p></div>",
  "Advantages & Disadvantages Essays": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Advantages and Disadvantages Essays</strong><br><span>Weigh the good against the bad in a clear, balanced way.</span></div><p>This essay type asks you to describe the benefits and drawbacks of a situation. Watch the wording carefully. If it asks whether the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, you must also give an opinion. If it simply asks to discuss both, a balanced answer is enough.</p><p>Organise by giving advantages and disadvantages their own paragraphs.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the question to check whether an opinion is required.</li><li>Introduce the topic and preview both sides.</li><li>Explain the main advantages in one paragraph with an example.</li><li>Explain the main disadvantages in the next paragraph.</li><li>Conclude by stating which side is stronger if an opinion is asked.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Model sentence: Studying abroad offers world-class training, yet it separates students from family; overall, the long-term career benefits outweigh this temporary cost.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many candidates lose marks by ignoring the opinion when the question demands it. Matching your answer exactly to the wording protects your Task Response score and shows the examiner you understood the task precisely.</p></div>",
  "Two-Part Questions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Two-Part Questions</strong><br><span>Answer both questions fully, or lose half your marks.</span></div><p>Two-part questions, sometimes called direct questions, ask two separate things. For example: Why do people move to cities, and what problems does this create. There is no agree or disagree here. Your only job is to answer each question clearly and completely.</p><p>The safest structure gives one body paragraph to each question.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify the two questions and underline both.</li><li>Paraphrase them together in your introduction.</li><li>Answer the first question in the first body paragraph.</li><li>Answer the second question in the second body paragraph.</li><li>Combine both answers briefly in your conclusion.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Model sentence: Many rural Liberians move to Monrovia seeking work and schooling, but this rapid growth strains housing and clean water supplies.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The most common mistake is answering only one part. Because Task Response rewards a full answer, addressing both questions with equal depth is the surest way to reach band 7 in this essay type.</p></div>",
  "Linking Words": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Linking Words</strong><br><span>Linking words are the road signs that guide your reader through your ideas.</span></div><p>Linking words, also called cohesive devices, connect sentences and paragraphs so your writing flows smoothly. They signal addition, contrast, cause, result, and sequence. Used well, they make your argument easy to follow. Used too much or wrongly, they sound mechanical and can lower your score.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Addition: furthermore, in addition, moreover.</li><li>Contrast: however, on the other hand, nevertheless.</li><li>Cause and result: therefore, as a result, consequently.</li><li>Examples: for instance, such as, to illustrate.</li><li>Sequence: firstly, secondly, finally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Model sentence: Solar power is clean; however, the batteries remain expensive, and therefore many families still rely on generators.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Coherence and Cohesion is one full quarter of your Task 2 score. A varied, accurate range of linking words shows the examiner you can organise ideas, but remember that natural, well-placed connectors score higher than a long list crammed into every line.</p></div>",
  "Cohesion & Coherence": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Cohesion and Coherence</strong><br><span>Coherence is clear thinking; cohesion is clear connecting.</span></div><p>Coherence means your ideas are logically ordered and easy to understand. Cohesion means the sentences and paragraphs are linked smoothly through referencing, linking words, and repeated key ideas. Together they form one full marking criterion in Writing Task 2, so they deserve real attention.</p><p>Good cohesion goes beyond linking words. It also uses pronouns and synonyms to avoid repetition.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Plan a logical order for your ideas before writing.</li><li>Give each paragraph one clear central idea.</li><li>Open paragraphs with a topic sentence.</li><li>Use pronouns like it, this, and they to refer back naturally.</li><li>Replace repeated nouns with synonyms where possible.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Model sentence: Cassava is a key crop in Liberia. It grows in poor soil, which makes this staple reliable during difficult seasons.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many strong candidates have good vocabulary but jump between ideas. Smooth coherence and cohesion make your essay feel effortless to read, and that ease is exactly what pushes you from band 6 into band 7.</p></div>",
  "Speaking Part 1": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Speaking Part 1</strong><br><span>The warm-up round where you make a calm, friendly first impression.</span></div><p>Speaking Part 1 lasts about four to five minutes. The examiner asks simple questions about familiar topics such as your home, work, studies, hobbies, and daily routine. Answers should be short but complete, usually two or three sentences, with a reason or detail added.</p><p>Do not give one-word answers, and do not memorise long speeches.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Listen carefully to the exact question.</li><li>Give a direct answer in the first sentence.</li><li>Add a reason, example, or feeling to extend it.</li><li>Keep your tone relaxed and natural.</li><li>Stop when your answer feels complete, without rambling.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Question: Do you enjoy cooking? Sample answer: Yes, I really do. I often prepare jollof rice for my family on Sundays because cooking together helps us relax after a busy week.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Part 1 sets the tone for your whole test. Comfortable, extended answers on easy topics build your confidence and show the examiner early that you can speak fluently, which shapes their impression of everything that follows.</p></div>",
  "Speaking Part 2 Cue Cards": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Speaking Part 2 Cue Cards</strong><br><span>One minute to prepare, two minutes to shine on a single topic.</span></div><p>In Part 2 the examiner gives you a card with a topic and three or four points to cover. You get one minute to make notes, then you speak alone for one to two minutes. This is your chance to show a long, connected stretch of speech.</p><p>Use the bullet points as a natural map, and keep talking until the examiner stops you.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the card and note one keyword for each point.</li><li>Choose a real memory so ideas come easily.</li><li>Start by introducing the topic in one sentence.</li><li>Cover each bullet point in order, adding details.</li><li>End with how you felt or why it mattered.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Card: Describe a place you like to visit. Sample opening: I would like to talk about Providence Island in Monrovia, a quiet historical spot by the water where I often go to think and take photos.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Part 2 tests whether you can speak at length without support. Filling the full two minutes with organised, detailed speech gives the examiner plenty of language to reward across fluency, vocabulary, and grammar.</p></div>",
  "Speaking Part 3 Discussions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Speaking Part 3 Discussions</strong><br><span>The deeper conversation where you explain, compare, and predict.</span></div><p>Part 3 follows the cue card and lasts four to five minutes. The questions become more abstract, asking about society, the future, or opinions rather than your personal life. The examiner wants developed answers with reasons, examples, and comparisons, not short replies.</p><p>Think of it as a mature discussion between two adults exploring ideas.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Give your direct opinion first.</li><li>Explain the reason behind it.</li><li>Support it with an example or comparison.</li><li>Consider the other side or the future where useful.</li><li>Use phrases to buy time, such as that is an interesting question.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Question: How can governments reduce youth unemployment? Sample answer: I believe vocational training is essential. For example, teaching skills like carpentry or coding gives young people income even when formal jobs are limited.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Part 3 is where higher bands are won or lost. Developing abstract ideas with clear reasoning shows the range and flexibility that separate a band 6 speaker from a confident band 7 or 8 speaker.</p></div>",
  "Expanding Answers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Expanding Answers</strong><br><span>Turn a short reply into a rich, natural answer the examiner can reward.</span></div><p>Expanding means adding more to a basic answer so you show off your English. A short yes or no gives the examiner nothing to score. By adding reasons, examples, and details, you create the extended speech that higher bands require, without sounding memorised.</p><p>A simple habit is to keep asking yourself why and can I give an example.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Answer the question directly first.</li><li>Add because to give a reason.</li><li>Add for example to give a specific detail.</li><li>Add a personal feeling or a comparison.</li><li>Stop before the answer becomes repetitive.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Short: Yes, I like music. Expanded: Yes, I love music, especially highlife and afrobeats, because it lifts my mood. For instance, I always play it while studying, and it helps me concentrate.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Fluency and coherence reward candidates who speak at length without long pauses. Learning to expand every answer gives you more chances to use good vocabulary and grammar, which raises your score in all four speaking criteria.</p></div>",
  "Pronunciation Skills": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Pronunciation Skills</strong><br><span>Clear speech that is easy to understand beats a fake accent every time.</span></div><p>Pronunciation is one of the four speaking marks. The examiner is not looking for a British or American accent. They want speech that is clear and easy to follow, with good stress, rhythm, and intonation. Your natural West African accent is completely acceptable as long as you are understood.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Word stress: say important syllables more strongly, as in imPORtant.</li><li>Sentence stress: emphasise the key words that carry meaning.</li><li>Intonation: let your voice rise and fall instead of staying flat.</li><li>Clear sounds: finish word endings such as the final t or s.</li><li>Pausing: pause at natural points, not in the middle of ideas.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Practice sentence: I really enjoyed the FESTival because the MUsic and the FOOD were wonderful. Stress the capital syllables to sound natural and clear.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Even excellent grammar is wasted if the examiner cannot follow you. Clear stress and intonation make your ideas land, and they directly lift the pronunciation band, so practise reading aloud daily and record yourself to check.</p></div>",
  "Fluency Development": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Fluency Development</strong><br><span>Fluency is speaking smoothly, not speaking fast.</span></div><p>Fluency means talking at a steady, natural pace without too many long pauses or repeated corrections. It does not mean rushing. Examiners value speakers who keep going and connect their ideas, even if they make small mistakes along the way. Confidence and practice build fluency more than anything else.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Practise speaking English aloud every day, even alone.</li><li>Use filler phrases like well and let me think to avoid silence.</li><li>Do not stop to correct every small error, keep the flow.</li><li>Read short texts aloud to build speaking rhythm.</li><li>Record yourself weekly and notice your improvement.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Sample answer: Well, that is a good question. I would say that reading helps me most, because, you know, it builds my vocabulary and keeps my mind active every day.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Fluency and coherence is a full quarter of your speaking score. Long, hesitant pauses drag it down, while smooth, connected speech lifts it. Daily speaking practice, even with a friend or a mirror, is the fastest route to a higher band.</p></div>",
  "Vocabulary for Speaking": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Vocabulary for Speaking</strong><br><span>The right words, used naturally, lift every answer you give.</span></div><p>Lexical resource is the vocabulary mark in your speaking test. The examiner wants a range of words used accurately, including some less common ones, idioms, and topic-specific terms. You do not need difficult words in every sentence. You need the right word in the right place, spoken naturally.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Learn words in topic groups, such as work, travel, and technology.</li><li>Use collocations, natural word pairs like heavy rain and make a decision.</li><li>Add a few idioms you truly understand, such as once in a while.</li><li>Paraphrase when you forget a word instead of freezing.</li><li>Avoid overusing basic words like good, nice, and thing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Basic: The market was good. Stronger: The market was bustling and vibrant, packed with traders selling everything from fresh cassava to colourful fabrics.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A wide, accurate vocabulary is a quarter of your speaking score and helps you sound precise and interesting. Building a personal word bank around common IELTS topics gives you ready language for test day, so you never run out of things to say.</p></div>",
  "Understanding Accents": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Understanding Accents</strong><br><span>The IELTS Listening test speaks in many voices, and your ear must welcome them all.</span></div><p>IELTS Listening features a range of native accents: British, Australian, American, Canadian and New Zealand. At Band 7+, you are expected to follow meaning even when the pronunciation is unfamiliar, without letting one strange-sounding word derail an entire section.</p><p>The key is training your ear through repeated, varied exposure rather than panicking at first contact.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Listen daily to BBC, ABC Australia and NPR so multiple accents become normal.</li><li>Notice how vowels shift: British <em>can't</em> sounds like <em>cahnt</em>, American like <em>cant</em>.</li><li>Focus on the whole phrase for meaning, not one troublesome sound.</li><li>Shadow speakers aloud, copying their rhythm and stress.</li><li>Re-listen to transcripts to link the sound you heard with the word you read.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An Australian speaker says <em>the timetable is on the day after tomorrow</em>, pronouncing <em>day</em> closer to <em>die</em>. A trained listener still catches the scheduling detail and writes <em>Thursday</em> without hesitation.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>You cannot choose which accent your test uses. Learners in Liberia who prepare with only one variety often lose marks in Sections 3 and 4. Broad exposure turns unfamiliar accents into a minor detail instead of a wall between you and Band 8.</p></div>",
  "Predicting Answers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Predicting Answers</strong><br><span>Strong listeners hear the answer coming before it arrives.</span></div><p>Prediction means using the question and its context to guess what type of answer you need before the audio plays. This sharpens your focus so that when the correct information appears, you recognise it instantly instead of scrambling to catch up.</p><p>It is an active strategy: you read ahead, anticipate, then confirm.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>In the preparation seconds, read every question and underline keywords.</li><li>Decide the grammar needed: a number, a name, a plural noun, a verb?</li><li>Predict likely content from the topic, for example a market survey suggests prices or dates.</li><li>Watch the word limit so your answer fits the gap exactly.</li><li>Listen for paraphrase, since the audio rarely repeats the exact question words.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A gap reads: <em>The delivery arrives every ______ morning.</em> You predict a day of the week. When the speaker says <em>stock comes in on Wednesdays</em>, you confidently write <em>Wednesday</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The audio plays only once. Prediction buys you time and reduces surprise, letting you fill gaps calmly. Band 7+ candidates almost always predict, because it converts frantic guessing into confident recognition and prevents one missed answer from spoiling the next.</p></div>",
  "Identifying Opinions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Identifying Opinions</strong><br><span>The hardest listening marks are lost not on facts, but on who thinks what.</span></div><p>Many higher-band questions ask you to identify a speaker's opinion, attitude or degree of certainty. This is challenging because opinions are signalled by tone and subtle language, not clear labels, and speakers often change their minds mid-sentence.</p><p>You must track agreement, doubt, and disagreement across a conversation.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Listen for opinion signposts: <em>I reckon</em>, <em>to be honest</em>, <em>I'm not so sure</em>.</li><li>Separate fact from feeling by asking whether it can be proven or is a view.</li><li>Track changes of mind: <em>Actually, on second thought...</em> reverses an earlier claim.</li><li>Note qualifiers like <em>slightly</em>, <em>definitely</em> or <em>possibly</em> that show strength of opinion.</li><li>Match the final settled opinion, not the first one mentioned.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two students discuss a project. One says, <em>I thought the survey was pointless at first, but honestly it saved us.</em> The correct answer is that she now values it, despite the initial doubt.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Section 3 discussions between students and tutors depend heavily on opinion. Catching the shift from doubt to agreement separates Band 6 from Band 8 listeners, who hear not just words but the attitude carried inside them.</p></div>",
  "Following Lectures": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Following Lectures</strong><br><span>Section 4 is one long academic talk with no pauses, and it rewards the organised mind.</span></div><p>The final listening section is a monologue, usually a university-style lecture. There is no break to reset, so you must follow the structure of the talk, recognising how ideas are introduced, developed and connected across several minutes.</p><p>Understanding the shape of the lecture matters as much as catching each word.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Catch the opening, where the speaker previews the main sections.</li><li>Follow signposts: <em>firstly</em>, <em>moving on</em>, <em>a further point</em>, <em>finally</em>.</li><li>Listen for definitions and examples that explain key terms.</li><li>Keep your eyes moving down the question list to stay in sync with the talk.</li><li>If you miss one answer, let it go and lock onto the next signpost.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A lecture on cassava farming says, <em>Having covered planting, let us turn to storage.</em> That signpost tells you the next answers concern storage methods, so you shift your attention forward with the speaker.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Section 4 carries academic vocabulary and a fast pace with no dialogue to slow it down. Learners who follow the lecture's roadmap stay on track, while those who chase single words fall behind. Structure awareness is a true Band 7+ skill.</p></div>",
  "High-Band Listening Strategies": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 High-Band Listening Strategies</strong><br><span>Reaching Band 8 in Listening is about discipline and recovery, not just good ears.</span></div><p>High-band listening combines several habits: precise reading of questions, sharp prediction, spelling accuracy, and the ability to recover instantly after a missed answer. Small technical errors, not comprehension gaps, are what usually cost strong candidates their extra half band.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read ahead constantly so you always know the next question.</li><li>Listen for paraphrase and synonyms, the test's favourite trap.</li><li>Watch for distractors: a number or name that is corrected moments later.</li><li>Write answers cleanly and check spelling and singular or plural forms.</li><li>Never freeze on a lost answer; move on immediately to protect the rest.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A speaker says, <em>The room holds forty, sorry, fourteen people.</em> The distractor is <em>forty</em>; the correct answer is <em>fourteen</em>. High-band listeners catch the self-correction and write the second number.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>At the top end, comprehension is rarely the problem; carelessness is. A misspelled word or a missed correction quietly drops your score. Building these habits protects every mark you already earn and pushes you securely past Band 7.</p></div>",
  "Advanced Listening Review": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Advanced Listening Review</strong><br><span>One module, many skills; now let us weave them into a single confident performance.</span></div><p>This review pulls together everything from Module 11: accents, prediction, opinion tracking, lecture-following and high-band discipline. The goal is to practise them together under realistic timing, so the strategies become automatic rather than something you think about mid-test.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Sit a full four-section practice test without stopping the audio.</li><li>Apply prediction before each section and opinion-tracking in Section 3.</li><li>Mark your answers, then study every error to find its cause.</li><li>Re-listen to missed parts with the transcript to close the gap.</li><li>Log recurring weaknesses, such as spelling or plurals, and drill them.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">After a mock test, a learner in Monrovia finds three errors were all missed plural endings, like writing <em>ticket</em> for <em>tickets</em>. She adds a final plural check to her routine and gains a half band.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Isolated practice builds skills; integrated review builds scores. Reviewing mistakes honestly is the fastest route from Band 6 to Band 8, because it turns each error into a targeted lesson instead of a repeated loss.</p></div>",
  "Critical Reading": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Critical Reading</strong><br><span>Band 8 readers do not just absorb a text; they interrogate it.</span></div><p>Critical reading means engaging actively with a passage: recognising the writer's purpose, distinguishing fact from opinion, and noticing how evidence is used to build an argument. Instead of reading passively, you question what the author claims and why.</p><p>This deeper engagement is exactly what harder question types reward.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Identify the writer's purpose: to inform, argue, warn or compare.</li><li>Separate proven facts from the writer's personal judgement.</li><li>Notice cautious language such as <em>may</em>, <em>tends to</em> or <em>arguably</em>.</li><li>Track how each paragraph supports the central claim.</li><li>Question assumptions rather than accepting every statement at face value.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A passage states, <em>Critics argue that rapid urbanisation strains services, though evidence remains limited.</em> A critical reader notes this is a reported opinion with a hedge, not an established fact, and answers accordingly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Higher-band questions on opinion, inference and matching demand that you read beneath the surface. Passive readers miss the writer's stance and choose wrong answers. Critical reading gives you the awareness needed to handle the toughest questions with confidence.</p></div>",
  "Speed Reading Techniques": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Speed Reading Techniques</strong><br><span>You have three passages and sixty minutes; reading every word is a luxury you cannot afford.</span></div><p>Speed reading is the skill of moving quickly through text to find what you need without losing comprehension. The two core techniques are skimming, for the general idea, and scanning, for specific details, and knowing when to use each saves precious minutes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Skim first: read the title, opening lines and each paragraph's first sentence.</li><li>Build a mental map of where each topic sits in the passage.</li><li>Scan for keywords, names, dates and numbers when hunting a specific answer.</li><li>Slow down only on the exact lines that contain the answer.</li><li>Avoid re-reading whole paragraphs once you know the layout.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Asked when a company was founded, you scan for a four-digit year rather than reading the paragraph, spot <em>1987</em> in the second line, and move on within seconds.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many capable readers lose marks simply because they run out of time. Skimming and scanning let you spend your minutes where the marks are. For Band 7+, efficient reading is as important as understanding, because unanswered questions score nothing.</p></div>",
  "Inference Questions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Inference Questions</strong><br><span>Some answers are never written down; you must read what the text implies.</span></div><p>Inference questions ask you to draw a logical conclusion that the passage suggests but does not state directly. The answer is supported by the text, yet you will not find it word for word, so you must reason carefully from the clues given.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Find the relevant section using keywords from the question.</li><li>Read closely for what is implied, not merely what is stated.</li><li>Check that your conclusion is genuinely supported by the wording.</li><li>Reject options that go further than the text allows.</li><li>Beware answers that are true in life but not proven in this passage.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The text says, <em>She packed an umbrella and cancelled the outdoor stall.</em> You can infer bad weather was expected, even though rain is never mentioned. The clues justify the conclusion.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Inference is one of the most demanding reading skills and a clear marker of Band 7 and above. The trap is over-inferring or relying on outside knowledge. Learning to stay tied to the evidence, while still reading between the lines, protects you from confident but wrong answers.</p></div>",
  "Writer’s Opinion Questions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writer’s Opinion Questions</strong><br><span>Yes, No, Not Given: three small words that decide many hard-earned marks.</span></div><p>These questions test whether you can identify the writer's own views and claims. In the Yes/No/Not Given format, <em>Yes</em> means the statement agrees with the writer, <em>No</em> means it contradicts the writer, and <em>Not Given</em> means the writer simply does not express that view.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Locate the part of the text linked to the statement.</li><li>Ask whether the writer clearly agrees, disagrees, or says nothing.</li><li>Choose <em>Not Given</em> when the idea is absent, not when it feels unlikely.</li><li>Watch qualifiers like <em>all</em>, <em>never</em> or <em>only</em> that can flip agreement.</li><li>Judge the writer's opinion, not your own or common knowledge.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Statement: <em>Solar power is the best energy source.</em> The writer says solar is promising but discusses no comparison. Because no <em>best</em> claim appears, the answer is <em>Not Given</em>, not <em>Yes</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Candidates lose most marks here by confusing <em>No</em> with <em>Not Given</em>. Remember: <em>No</em> needs a contradiction in the text, while <em>Not Given</em> means silence. Mastering this distinction is one of the surest ways to lift your Reading band.</p></div>",
  "Complex Academic Passages": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Complex Academic Passages</strong><br><span>The third passage is dense, formal and unfamiliar, and it is where bands are won or lost.</span></div><p>Academic passages use long sentences, abstract ideas and specialised vocabulary drawn from science, history or social studies. You are not expected to know the topic, but you must decode dense text quickly and grasp the argument despite unfamiliar words.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Break long sentences into smaller parts to find the main clause.</li><li>Guess unknown words from context rather than stopping to worry.</li><li>Follow linking words like <em>however</em>, <em>therefore</em> and <em>despite</em> to track logic.</li><li>Focus on the overall argument, not every technical detail.</li><li>Use paragraph topic sentences to navigate the structure.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A sentence reads, <em>Although the hypothesis appeared robust, subsequent trials undermined its central premise.</em> Stripped down, it simply means the idea looked strong but later tests weakened it. You do not need to know <em>premise</em> precisely to grasp that.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Passage three intimidates many learners into panic and wasted time. Approaching dense text calmly, decoding structure and inferring vocabulary, keeps you productive. This composure under difficulty is precisely what distinguishes Band 8 readers.</p></div>",
  "Reading Time Management": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Reading Time Management</strong><br><span>Forty questions, sixty minutes, no extra time to transfer answers; the clock is your real opponent.</span></div><p>Time management means dividing your hour wisely across three passages so that every question gets attention. Since all passages carry equal marks but the third is hardest, a clear plan stops you from stranding easy marks at the end.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Allow about twenty minutes per passage, and watch the clock.</li><li>Answer easier questions first to secure marks quickly.</li><li>Do not let one hard question consume several minutes.</li><li>Mark and skip anything that stalls you, then return later.</li><li>Write answers directly on the answer sheet, as there is no extra transfer time.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A learner spends thirty-five minutes perfecting Passage 1 and rushes Passage 3, leaving eight questions blank. With even twenty-minute blocks, those eight questions would have been attempted and several likely correct.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Unlike Listening, the Reading test gives no separate transfer time, so pacing is entirely your responsibility. Strong readers still fail to reach Band 7 when they mismanage the clock. A disciplined plan ensures your ability, not the timer, decides your score.</p></div>",
  "Band 7+ Essay Structure": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Band 7+ Essay Structure</strong><br><span>A high-band essay is not just good English; it is clearly organised thought.</span></div><p>A Band 7+ Task 2 essay follows a clear four-paragraph shape: an introduction that paraphrases the question and states your position, two developed body paragraphs, and a conclusion that restates your view. Each paragraph carries one clear central idea supported by explanation and example.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Introduction: paraphrase the question and state your clear position.</li><li>Body 1: present your first main idea, explain it, then give an example.</li><li>Body 2: present a second main idea with its own support.</li><li>Use one clear topic sentence to open each body paragraph.</li><li>Conclusion: summarise your view without adding new points.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A topic sentence: <em>The most significant benefit of studying abroad is exposure to new ways of thinking.</em> The rest of the paragraph then explains this and offers a supporting example, keeping the idea unified.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Coherence and Cohesion is a full quarter of your Writing score. Examiners reward essays that are easy to follow, with each idea in its place. A reliable structure frees your mind to focus on ideas and language, which is how Band 6 writers reach Band 7 and beyond.</p></div>",
  "Advanced Vocabulary": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Advanced Vocabulary</strong><br><span>At Band 7+, it is not how many big words you know, but how precisely you use them.</span></div><p>Lexical Resource rewards a range of vocabulary used accurately and naturally. Advanced does not mean rare or showy; it means precise word choice, correct collocation and flexible phrasing. One misused fancy word harms your score more than a well-chosen simple one.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Prioritise precision: <em>a sharp rise</em> is better than a vague <em>big rise</em>.</li><li>Learn collocations: we say <em>make a decision</em>, not <em>do a decision</em>.</li><li>Use topic vocabulary naturally for areas like environment or education.</li><li>Paraphrase key words rather than repeating the question wording.</li><li>Avoid memorised idioms that sound forced or out of place.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Instead of <em>Many people think pollution is a very big problem,</em> write <em>Pollution is widely regarded as a pressing environmental challenge.</em> The upgrade is precise and natural, not merely longer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Examiners look for less common vocabulary used with control. Reaching for difficult words you cannot handle backfires, while accurate, well-collocated language lifts your band. Building vocabulary through reading and noting real usage is far more effective than memorising isolated word lists.</p></div>",
  "Academic Writing Style": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Academic Writing Style</strong><br><span>Task 2 is a formal essay, and its tone should sound like one.</span></div><p>Academic style means writing in a formal, objective and precise register. You avoid contractions, slang and overly personal or emotional language, favouring measured, well-supported statements. This tone signals maturity and control to the examiner.</p><p>It is the difference between a text message and an essay.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Avoid contractions: write <em>do not</em>, not <em>don't</em>.</li><li>Replace casual words: use <em>children</em>, not <em>kids</em>.</li><li>Prefer cautious claims: <em>this may lead to</em> rather than absolute certainty.</li><li>Limit personal phrases like <em>I think</em> to where a position is genuinely needed.</li><li>Keep tone impersonal and evidence-based, not emotional.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Casual: <em>Loads of kids don't wanna go to school and that's bad.</em> Academic: <em>A significant number of children are reluctant to attend school, which can have serious consequences.</em></div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>An informal tone quietly caps your score, no matter how good your ideas are. Examiners expect academic writing to read as academic. Adjusting your register is a quick, reliable way to sound more competent and to move confidently from Band 6 to Band 7+.</p></div>",
  "Complex Sentences": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Complex Sentences</strong><br><span>Grammatical range means showing the examiner you can do more than simple statements.</span></div><p>Complex sentences combine a main clause with one or more dependent clauses, often using words like <em>although</em>, <em>because</em>, <em>which</em> or <em>while</em>. They let you express relationships between ideas, and using them accurately is essential for the Grammatical Range and Accuracy criterion.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Use subordinators such as <em>although</em>, <em>since</em>, <em>whereas</em> and <em>if</em>.</li><li>Add relative clauses with <em>which</em>, <em>who</em> and <em>that</em>.</li><li>Mix sentence lengths; not every sentence should be long.</li><li>Prioritise accuracy, since a correct simple sentence beats a broken complex one.</li><li>Punctuate carefully, especially commas around dependent clauses.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Simple: <em>Cities are growing. Housing is scarce.</em> Complex: <em>Although cities are growing rapidly, affordable housing remains scarce, which forces many families into overcrowded areas.</em></div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A band descriptor for 7 requires a variety of complex structures used with flexibility. Writing only simple sentences caps you at a lower band, but forcing complexity you cannot control causes errors. Balancing range with accuracy is the true mark of Band 7+ grammar.</p></div>",
  "Data Analysis for Task 1": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Data Analysis for Task 1</strong><br><span>Task 1 is not about every number; it is about the story the data tells.</span></div><p>Academic Task 1 asks you to describe a chart, graph, table or process. High-band responses do not list every figure. Instead, they identify the main trends and key features, group related data, and support the overview with selected, accurate numbers.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Study the visual and note the biggest changes and comparisons.</li><li>Write an overview stating the main trends, with no specific data.</li><li>Group similar categories together instead of describing each in turn.</li><li>Support each group with one or two well-chosen figures.</li><li>Use trend language such as <em>rose sharply</em>, <em>plateaued</em> or <em>declined steadily</em>.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Overview: <em>Overall, rice consumption rose steadily across the period, while imported wheat fell after 2010.</em> A body sentence then adds detail: <em>Rice climbed from 40 to 65 kilograms per person.</em></div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A clear overview is the single most important feature of a Band 7 Task 1 answer; without it, your score is capped. Selecting and grouping data, rather than listing it, shows the analytical control examiners reward and keeps you within the time limit.</p></div>",
  "High-Band Sample Essays": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 High-Band Sample Essays</strong><br><span>Learning from strong models is one of the fastest ways to raise your own writing.</span></div><p>Studying Band 8 and 9 sample essays shows you how the criteria come alive on the page. Rather than copying, you analyse how successful writers structure arguments, link ideas, choose vocabulary and control grammar, then apply those techniques to your own work.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read a high-band model and identify its clear position and structure.</li><li>Underline linking words and note how paragraphs connect.</li><li>Highlight precise vocabulary and useful collocations to borrow.</li><li>Notice how ideas are explained and supported with examples.</li><li>Write your own essay on a similar topic, applying what you observed.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In a model essay, a learner spots the phrase <em>This trend can be attributed largely to...</em> and adapts it for cause-and-effect essays, replacing her weaker <em>This happens because of...</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many learners write repeatedly without knowing what excellent looks like, so they repeat the same mistakes. Analysing high-band essays gives you a concrete target and a bank of techniques. Combined with practice, this is one of the most efficient paths from Band 6 to Band 8.</p></div>",
  "Common Writing Mistakes": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Common Writing Mistakes</strong><br><span>Sometimes the fastest way up is to stop losing marks you should keep.</span></div><p>Even capable writers repeat the same errors that hold their scores down. Recognising these common mistakes, in task response, grammar, vocabulary and structure, lets you correct them deliberately and protect the marks your ideas already deserve.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Not answering the whole question, or missing part of a two-part task.</li><li>Writing under the 250-word minimum for Task 2.</li><li>Memorised phrases and off-topic examples that examiners penalise.</li><li>Repeating the same words instead of paraphrasing.</li><li>Weak paragraphing, with several ideas crammed together.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A prompt asks about causes and solutions, but the essay discusses only causes. Even with excellent English, the response cannot reach Band 7 because it fails to address half the task.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Task Response is a full quarter of your score, and these avoidable errors quietly drain it. Checking your essay against a short list of common mistakes before you finish is a simple habit that reliably lifts your band without needing any new vocabulary or grammar.</p></div>",
  "Task Achievement Strategies": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Task Achievement Strategies</strong><br><span>The first question the examiner asks is simple: did you actually do the task?</span></div><p>Task Achievement, or Task Response, measures how fully and accurately you answer the exact question set. It rewards addressing every part of the prompt, taking a clear position, and developing your ideas with relevant support, all within the required length.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the prompt twice and underline every part you must cover.</li><li>Identify the essay type: opinion, discussion, problem-solution or two-part.</li><li>State a clear position and keep it consistent throughout.</li><li>Develop each main idea with explanation and a relevant example.</li><li>Check before finishing that you answered fully and met the word count.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A prompt asks: <em>Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?</em> A high-band answer clearly states which side is stronger, rather than simply listing both without ever giving a verdict.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>You can write fluent, accurate English and still miss Band 7 if you do not fully answer the question. Task Achievement is where many strong writers lose marks. Directly and completely addressing the prompt is the foundation on which every other Writing skill rests.</p></div>",
  "Speaking Like a Native": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Speaking Like a Native</strong><br><span>Fluency is not about accent, it is about flow.</span></div><p>Speaking like a native means your English sounds natural, connected, and confident, even when your accent is proudly West African. Examiners do not lower your score for a Liberian or Nigerian accent. They reward smooth linking, natural word stress, and everyday expressions used correctly.</p><p>The goal is to sound like a fluent speaker who thinks in English, not one translating word by word.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Link words together so sounds blend, for example say pick-it-up as one flow.</li><li>Use contractions naturally: Im, dont, weve, theyre.</li><li>Add discourse markers like actually, to be honest, and I mean.</li><li>Vary your pace, slowing slightly to stress key ideas.</li><li>Shadow native audio daily, copying rhythm and intonation.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Instead of I like the market because there are many people, say To be honest, I really love the Waterside market, its always buzzing with life and you can find just about anything there.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Natural, connected speech signals genuine fluency to the examiner, lifting your Fluency and Coherence score toward Band 7 and above without needing to hide your identity.</p></div>",
  "Advanced Topic Discussions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Advanced Topic Discussions</strong><br><span>Part 3 rewards depth, not just longer answers.</span></div><p>Advanced topic discussions happen mainly in Speaking Part 3, where the examiner asks broader, more abstract questions about society, technology, education, and change. You are expected to analyse, compare, and give balanced opinions rather than simple personal answers.</p><p>Strong candidates extend ideas with reasons, examples, and consequences.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>State your position clearly in one sentence.</li><li>Give a reason using because, since, or the main factor is.</li><li>Add an example or real situation to support it.</li><li>Consider the other side with however or on the other hand.</li><li>Round off with a short consequence or prediction.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Question: Should governments invest more in public transport? I firmly believe they should, mainly because reliable transport connects rural communities to jobs. In Monrovia, for instance, better roads would help traders reach markets faster. That said, funding is limited, so priorities must be balanced carefully.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Part 3 is where Band 7 to 9 candidates separate themselves. Developing ideas with structure and balance shows the range of language and reasoning examiners look for.</p></div>",
  "Abstract Ideas": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Abstract Ideas</strong><br><span>Talking about concepts, not just concrete things.</span></div><p>Abstract ideas are topics you cannot touch, such as freedom, success, tradition, justice, or happiness. Advanced Speaking often asks you to discuss these, and many learners freeze because the questions feel philosophical.</p><p>The trick is to anchor abstract concepts to concrete examples from your own life and community.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Define the concept briefly in your own words.</li><li>Give a concrete example that makes it real.</li><li>Explain why it matters to people or society.</li><li>Use hedging language like it seems to me or in many ways.</li><li>Connect it back to the question to stay on topic.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Question: What does success mean to you? To me, success is not only about money, it is about impact. For example, a teacher in my town who helped many students pass their exams is far more successful, in my view, than someone who is simply wealthy but forgotten.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Handling abstract questions calmly proves you can express complex thought in English, a key requirement for Band 7 and higher on the Speaking test.</p></div>",
  "Debate and Argument Skills": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Debate and Argument Skills</strong><br><span>Defend a view and weigh the other side with confidence.</span></div><p>Debate and argument skills help you present opinions persuasively while acknowledging counter-arguments. In Speaking Part 3, examiners may push back or ask if you agree, and a strong candidate stays composed and structured.</p><p>You are not judged on which side you pick, only on how well you argue it.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Take a clear stance early.</li><li>Support it with two solid reasons.</li><li>Acknowledge the opposing view fairly.</li><li>Rebut it politely with while that is true or even so.</li><li>Restate your position to close strongly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Some people argue that social media harms young people, and I understand that concern. While it is true that it can be distracting, I would argue the benefits outweigh the risks, since it gives young Liberians access to education and global opportunities they never had before.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Confident, balanced argumentation demonstrates advanced grammar, linking, and vocabulary all at once, exactly the range that pushes Speaking scores into the highest bands.</p></div>",
  "Idioms and Collocations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Idioms and Collocations</strong><br><span>Natural word partnerships that make you sound fluent.</span></div><p>Collocations are words that naturally go together, like make a decision or heavy rain. Idioms are fixed expressions whose meaning is not literal, like once in a blue moon. Using them correctly signals near-native fluency.</p><p>Use them sparingly and accurately, because a forced or wrong idiom hurts more than it helps.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Learn collocations as whole chunks, not single words.</li><li>Common verbs: make, do, take, have, and get carry many collocations.</li><li>Use idioms only when you are sure of their meaning.</li><li>Everyday idioms work best: over the moon, a piece of cake, hit the books.</li><li>One or two well-placed idioms per answer is plenty.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">When I finally passed my exams, I was over the moon. Studying was no piece of cake, but I made a real effort and it paid off in the end.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The Band 7 to 9 descriptors specifically reward idiomatic language and skilful collocation. Accurate, natural chunks lift your Lexical Resource score noticeably.</p></div>",
  "Pronunciation for Band 8+": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Pronunciation for Band 8+</strong><br><span>Clarity and rhythm matter far more than a British accent.</span></div><p>Pronunciation for Band 8 and above is about being easy to understand while using features like word stress, sentence stress, intonation, and connected speech. You keep your West African identity, you just control your delivery.</p><p>Examiners want effortless comprehension, not imitation.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Stress the correct syllable in longer words, like comFORtable.</li><li>Emphasise the key content words in each sentence.</li><li>Use rising intonation for lists and questions, falling for statements.</li><li>Pronounce word endings clearly, especially s and ed sounds.</li><li>Pause at natural points instead of after every word.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Practise: I have deCIDed to STUDy aBROAD next YEAR. Stress the capitalised parts, glide through the rest, and let your voice fall gently at the end to sound calm and certain.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Pronunciation is a full quarter of your Speaking score. Mastering stress and intonation, not accent removal, is the realistic path to Band 8 for West African learners.</p></div>",
  "Speaking Confidence Building": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Speaking Confidence Building</strong><br><span>Nerves fade when preparation becomes a habit.</span></div><p>Speaking confidence building is about training your mind and mouth so anxiety does not block your fluency on exam day. Many capable candidates underperform simply because they freeze in front of the examiner.</p><p>Confidence comes from repeated, realistic practice, not from wishing.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Practise speaking aloud daily, even to yourself.</li><li>Record and listen back to spot habits and fillers.</li><li>Simulate the test with a friend acting as examiner.</li><li>Prepare phrases to buy time, like that is an interesting question.</li><li>Breathe slowly before answering to steady your voice.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">If a question surprises you, stay calm and say: That is a good question, let me think for a moment. Well, I suppose the main thing is... This keeps you fluent while your mind organises the answer.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A calm, confident delivery lets your true English ability shine through. Confidence protects your Fluency and Coherence score from being dragged down by nerves.</p></div>",
  "IELTS Exam Strategies": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 IELTS Exam Strategies</strong><br><span>Working smart beats working hard on test day.</span></div><p>IELTS exam strategies are the proven tactics that help you score more from the English you already know. Each section rewards specific techniques, from skimming in Reading to signposting in Writing.</p><p>Strategy turns effort into marks by removing avoidable mistakes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read every instruction carefully, including word limits.</li><li>Answer easy questions first, then return to harder ones.</li><li>Never leave a blank, an educated guess costs nothing.</li><li>Underline keywords in questions before searching for answers.</li><li>Keep an eye on the clock for every section.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In Reading, if a question says NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS, writing three words scores zero even if the meaning is right. Always count your words before moving on, a simple habit that saves easy marks.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Two candidates with equal English often score differently because one used strategy. Smart tactics can raise your overall band by half a point or more.</p></div>",
  "Time Management": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Time Management</strong><br><span>The clock is your toughest examiner.</span></div><p>Time management means spending the right amount of time on each task so you finish every section. Many candidates lose marks not from weak English but from running out of time before completing questions.</p><p>Knowing your pace in advance keeps panic away.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Learn the time limit for each section before test day.</li><li>In Writing, give Task 1 about twenty minutes and Task 2 about forty.</li><li>In Reading, allow roughly twenty minutes per passage.</li><li>Set mini deadlines and move on if you are stuck.</li><li>Leave a few minutes to check answers at the end.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In Academic Writing, a candidate who spends thirty minutes perfecting Task 1 often rushes Task 2, which carries double the marks. Discipline yourself: when twenty minutes pass, move to Task 2 no matter what.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Unanswered questions are guaranteed zeros. Good time management ensures your knowledge actually reaches the answer sheet, protecting your band across all four skills.</p></div>",
  "Mock Tests": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Mock Tests</strong><br><span>Rehearse the real thing before it counts.</span></div><p>Mock tests are full practice exams taken under real conditions, with strict timing and no interruptions. They build stamina, reveal weaknesses, and remove the shock of the actual test day.</p><p>A mock test is the closest you can get to the real exam before you pay for it.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Choose an official or trusted practice test.</li><li>Sit it in one quiet block with strict timing.</li><li>Do all four skills to build real endurance.</li><li>Mark it honestly against band descriptors.</li><li>Record your scores and track improvement over weeks.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Set aside a Saturday morning, switch off your phone, and complete Listening, Reading, and Writing back to back, just as on test day. You will quickly discover whether fatigue affects your concentration in the final section.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Mock tests turn theory into readiness. Candidates who take several full mocks walk into the exam calm and familiar, which reliably lifts performance across every band.</p></div>",
  "Error Analysis": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Error Analysis</strong><br><span>Your mistakes are a map to your next band.</span></div><p>Error analysis is the habit of studying your own mistakes to understand why they happened and how to prevent them. Repeating practice without reviewing errors simply cements bad habits.</p><p>Every corrected error is a small permanent gain in your score.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>After each test, list every wrong answer.</li><li>Group errors by type, such as grammar, spelling, or misreading.</li><li>Find the pattern behind the most frequent mistakes.</li><li>Study the correct rule or vocabulary for each.</li><li>Re-test the same skill to confirm the fix.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Keep an error log: Wrote he go instead of he goes, subject verb agreement. Missed a Listening answer because of a plural s. Seeing the same category repeat tells you exactly what to drill next.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Targeted correction is the fastest route upward. Fixing a few recurring error patterns often raises Writing and Grammar scores more than hours of general study.</p></div>",
  "Band Score Improvement": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Band Score Improvement</strong><br><span>Small, focused gains add up to a higher band.</span></div><p>Band score improvement is the deliberate process of moving from your current level to your target, one skill at a time. Because the overall band is an average, lifting your weakest section often gives the biggest jump.</p><p>Improvement is planned, measured, and repeatable, not accidental.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify your weakest skill from mock results.</li><li>Study the band descriptor for your target level.</li><li>Practise that skill intensively for two to three weeks.</li><li>Learn the exact features the next band requires.</li><li>Re-test and measure your progress against the goal.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A candidate scoring Listening 6, Reading 6, Writing 5.5, Speaking 7 should focus on Writing. Raising it to 6.5 lifts the overall average more efficiently than pushing an already strong Speaking to 7.5.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Many university and immigration programmes require a specific band. Understanding how the average works helps you reach that threshold with the least wasted effort.</p></div>",
  "Exam Day Preparation & Computer-Based IELTS": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Exam Day Preparation & Computer-Based IELTS</strong><br><span>Be ready for the room, the rules, and the screen.</span></div><p>Exam day preparation covers everything from documents to nerves, while computer-based IELTS adds screen skills like typing and on-screen navigation. Knowing what to expect removes surprises that cost marks.</p><p>Both paper and computer tests share the same content, only the format differs.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Pack your ID and confirmation the night before.</li><li>Sleep well and eat a proper breakfast.</li><li>Arrive early to settle your nerves.</li><li>For computer IELTS, practise typing and using the highlight and copy tools.</li><li>Watch the on-screen timer and word count in Writing.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">On computer-based IELTS, the screen shows your word count automatically, so you no longer count by hand. Practise typing essays beforehand so your speed matches your handwriting pace and you finish comfortably.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A candidate flustered by an unfamiliar computer interface can lose easy marks. Preparing for the exact format you booked lets your English, not the logistics, decide your score.</p></div>",
  "Academic vs General IELTS": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Academic vs General IELTS</strong><br><span>Same test, two versions, different purposes.</span></div><p>Academic and General Training IELTS share identical Listening and Speaking sections but differ in Reading and Writing. Choosing the right one matters because institutions accept only the version they require.</p><p>Academic suits universities and professional registration, General suits migration and work.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Listening and Speaking are the same in both versions.</li><li>Academic Reading uses journal and textbook style passages.</li><li>General Reading uses notices, adverts, and workplace texts.</li><li>Academic Writing Task 1 describes a chart, graph, or diagram.</li><li>General Writing Task 1 is a letter, formal or informal.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A Liberian student applying to a university abroad takes Academic and describes a bar chart in Task 1. A worker migrating to Canada takes General and writes a letter to a landlord instead. Same skills, different tasks.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Sitting the wrong version can waste your fee and delay your plans. Confirm with your university or immigration office first, then prepare for the correct Reading and Writing tasks.</p></div>",
  "The IELTS Test: Academic vs General Training": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 The IELTS Test: Academic vs General Training</strong><br><span>Four skills, two versions, one score that decides your plans.</span></div><p>IELTS tests four skills in one sitting: Listening, Reading and Writing run back to back with no break, and Speaking is a face-to-face interview held on the same day or within seven days. The whole thing takes about two hours and forty-five minutes.</p><p>There are two versions. Listening and Speaking are <strong>identical</strong> in both. Only Reading and Writing change. Academic is for universities and professional registration; General Training is for migration, secondary school and work programmes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Listening</strong> – 30 minutes, 40 questions, the recording plays once only.</li><li><strong>Reading</strong> – 60 minutes, 40 questions, three passages, no extra time to copy answers.</li><li><strong>Writing</strong> – 60 minutes, two tasks; Task 2 is worth twice Task 1.</li><li><strong>Speaking</strong> – 11 to 14 minutes with a real examiner, in three parts.</li><li>Academic Reading uses journal and textbook passages; General Reading uses notices, adverts and workplace texts.</li><li>Academic Writing Task 1 describes a chart or process; General Writing Task 1 is a letter.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Musu wants a master's degree in Ghana, so she books <em>Academic</em>. Her cousin James is applying to move to Canada as a skilled worker, so he books <em>General Training</em>. They sit the same Listening and Speaking papers on the same morning and different Reading and Writing papers.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Test centres will not swap your version after you book, and a university will not accept a General Training score. Confirm the requirement in writing with your institution or immigration office <em>before</em> you pay the fee.</p></div>",
  "Understanding the Band Score System (0–9)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Understanding the Band Score System (0–9)</strong><br><span>Nine bands, half-band steps, and one rounding rule that surprises people.</span></div><p>Each of the four skills gets its own band from 0 to 9. Your <strong>overall band</strong> is the average of those four, rounded to the nearest half band. Band 6 is described as a competent user, Band 7 a good user, Band 8 a very good user.</p><p>The rounding rule is the part candidates miss. An average ending in .25 rounds <strong>up</strong> to the next half band; an average ending in .75 rounds <strong>up</strong> to the next whole band. Anything below .25 rounds down.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Bands are reported in whole and half steps: 6.0, 6.5, 7.0 and so on.</li><li>The overall band is an average, so one weak skill drags the whole score down.</li><li>Most universities ask for <strong>6.5 overall with no band below 6.0</strong>.</li><li>Listening and Reading are marked out of 40 and converted to a band; roughly 30/40 is about Band 7.</li><li>Writing and Speaking are marked by trained examiners against four published criteria, each worth 25%.</li><li>Your Test Report Form is valid for two years.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Listening 7.0, Reading 6.5, Writing 6.0, Speaking 6.5 adds to 26.0. Divided by four that is 6.5 exactly — overall Band 6.5. Now drop Speaking to 6.0: the total is 25.5, the average is 6.375, and that rounds down to <strong>6.0</strong>. Half a band in one skill moved the whole result.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Knowing the arithmetic tells you where to spend your study time. If three skills sit at 6.5 and one at 5.5, lifting the weakest skill is worth far more than polishing the strongest one.</p></div>",
  "Test-Day Logistics & Smart Preparation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Test-Day Logistics & Smart Preparation</strong><br><span>The band you get is partly decided before you write a word.</span></div><p>You must bring the <strong>same identity document</strong> you used to register — normally your passport. A different ID, even a valid one, can stop you sitting the test. Arrive early; latecomers are not admitted once the Listening recording starts.</p><p>Phones, smart watches and bags go into storage outside the room. Nothing is allowed on the desk except your ID, a pencil, an eraser and the water bottle the centre permits (label removed).</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Bring the exact ID used at registration, plus your confirmation email.</li><li>Arrive at least 30 minutes early and expect photo and fingerprint checks.</li><li>On the <strong>paper</strong> test you get 10 extra minutes to transfer Listening answers; Reading has none.</li><li>On the <strong>computer-delivered</strong> test you type answers directly, so there is no transfer time in Listening either.</li><li>Speaking may be scheduled on a different day — check your slot carefully.</li><li>Eat before you go. Listening, Reading and Writing run for nearly three hours with no break.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two candidates in Monrovia book the same date. One brings the national ID card although she registered with a passport, and is turned away at the door. The other checked her confirmation email the night before, arrived at 07:30 for an 08:00 start, and was seated calmly when the recording began.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>None of this is about English. It is the cheapest part of the exam to get right, and the most expensive to get wrong — a missed sitting means paying the full fee again and waiting weeks for the next date.</p></div>",
  "The Four Sections & Every Question Type": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 The Four Sections & Every Question Type</strong><br><span>Forty questions, thirty minutes, and the recording plays once.</span></div><p>IELTS Listening has four sections that get harder as they go. Section 1 is an everyday conversation between two speakers, such as booking a room. Section 2 is an everyday monologue, such as a tour guide. Section 3 is an academic discussion between up to four speakers. Section 4 is an academic lecture with no breaks in it.</p><p>You hear each recording <strong>once</strong>. Questions follow the order of the audio, so if you miss one, leave it and move to the next — chasing a lost answer costs you the two that follow.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Form, note, table and flow-chart completion</strong> – fill gaps with words from the audio.</li><li><strong>Multiple choice</strong> – one answer from three, or several from a longer list.</li><li><strong>Matching</strong> – link speakers or items to a list of options.</li><li><strong>Plan, map and diagram labelling</strong> – follow directions such as <em>opposite</em>, <em>beyond</em>, <em>at the far end</em>.</li><li><strong>Sentence completion and short answer</strong> – obey the stated word limit exactly.</li><li>Section 4 has no pause in the middle, so read all its questions during the introduction.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In Section 1 the speaker says <em>&ldquo;My surname is Kollie — K-O-L-L-I-E&rdquo;</em> and you write it on a booking form. In Section 4 a lecturer explains rice yields for two minutes without stopping, and you complete six notes in order. Same skill, very different pressure.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every section rewards a different habit. Knowing which one is coming lets you prepare the right habit in the ten seconds you are given before it starts, instead of discovering the format while the audio runs.</p></div>",
  "Strategies: Prediction, Keywords & Distractor Traps": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Strategies: Prediction, Keywords & Distractor Traps</strong><br><span>The audio never uses the words on your question paper.</span></div><p>Before each section you get a few seconds to read the questions. Use them to <strong>predict</strong>: what kind of word fits this gap — a number, a name, a day, a plural noun? A gap after <em>&ldquo;costs&rdquo;</em> needs money; a gap after <em>&ldquo;on&rdquo;</em> needs a day or date.</p><p>Then listen for <strong>meaning</strong>, not matching words. The question may say <em>&ldquo;the main problem&rdquo;</em> while the speaker says <em>&ldquo;the biggest difficulty we ran into&rdquo;</em>. Paraphrase is the point of the test.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Underline the keyword in each question and think of two ways to say it.</li><li>Predict the word class and the likely form before the audio starts.</li><li>Listen for correction signals: <em>actually</em>, <em>sorry</em>, <em>in fact</em>, <em>I meant</em>, <em>hold on</em>.</li><li>Listen for contrast signals: <em>however</em>, <em>although</em>, <em>but then again</em> — the answer usually follows them.</li><li>A number said first is often the distractor; the corrected one is the answer.</li><li>Never leave a blank. An unanswered question scores zero; a guess sometimes scores one.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">You hear: <em>&ldquo;The trip leaves at nine thirty… sorry, that's the old timetable, it's actually ten fifteen now.&rdquo;</em> A candidate who writes the first number they hear loses the mark. The signal word <em>sorry</em> was the whole question.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Most lost Listening marks are not vocabulary failures. They are candidates hearing the trap, writing it down, and moving on before the speaker finishes the sentence.</p></div>",
  "Spelling, Numbers & Transferring Answers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Spelling, Numbers & Transferring Answers</strong><br><span>A correct answer spelled wrong scores zero.</span></div><p>Listening and Reading answers are marked for spelling. British and American spellings are both accepted (<em>organise</em> or <em>organize</em>), but a misspelling is simply wrong. So is breaking the word limit: if the instruction says <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</strong>, a three-word answer scores nothing even when the meaning is right.</p><p>A hyphenated word such as <em>check-in</em> counts as one word. Numbers written as figures count as one. Writing everything in CAPITAL LETTERS is allowed and removes any doubt about your handwriting.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Learn to hear <strong>13 and 30, 14 and 40, 15 and 50</strong> — the stress moves to the end in the teens.</li><li>Dates can be written 21 March, 21st March or 21/03; be consistent.</li><li>Practise spelling names and places that are read out letter by letter.</li><li>Copy singular and plural exactly as the audio gives them; a missing <em>s</em> is a lost mark.</li><li>Paper test: you get <strong>10 minutes</strong> at the end to transfer Listening answers — use every one of them.</li><li>Computer test: there is no transfer time, so type into the box carefully the first time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The audio says <em>&ldquo;the fee is fifty dollars&rdquo;</em>. A candidate writes <em>$15</em>. Another writes <em>fifty dollar</em> where the limit was one word and a number. Both understood the recording perfectly. Both scored zero on that question.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Listening and Reading are each 40 marks converted straight to a band. Four careless spelling or transfer slips is roughly half a band — the exact gap that stops a 6.0 from becoming a 6.5.</p></div>",
  "Skimming, Scanning & Time Management": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Skimming, Scanning & Time Management</strong><br><span>Sixty minutes, three passages, forty questions — and no extra time at the end.</span></div><p>Reading is not a comprehension exercise you can take your time over. There are about 2,150 words to handle in an hour, and unlike Listening there is <strong>no transfer time</strong>: answers must already be on the answer sheet when the hour ends.</p><p>The passages get harder. Budget roughly <strong>17 minutes for Passage 1, 20 for Passage 2 and 23 for Passage 3</strong>, and move on when your time is up even if two questions are unanswered. All forty questions are worth one mark each, so an easy mark in Passage 3 is worth exactly as much as a hard one in Passage 1.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Skim</strong> for gist: read the title, the first and last sentence of each paragraph, then stop.</li><li><strong>Scan</strong> for detail: run your eye down the page hunting one specific thing — a date, a name, a capital letter, a number.</li><li>Read the questions before you read the passage properly; they tell you what to look for.</li><li>Most question types follow the order of the passage, so answers move downward.</li><li>Never read every word of a passage. There is not enough time and you are not marked on it.</li><li>Transfer each answer as you finish it, not at the end.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A candidate spends 28 minutes reading Passage 1 thoroughly and gets all 13 questions right. She then has 32 minutes for 27 questions and rushes both remaining passages, scoring 11. Total 24. A classmate who kept to the clock scored 10, 10 and 9 — total 29, a band higher, with weaker English.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>In IELTS Reading, time management <em>is</em> a reading skill. The examiner cannot see how well you understood a passage you never reached.</p></div>",
  "True / False / Not Given (and Yes / No / Not Given)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 True / False / Not Given (and Yes / No / Not Given)</strong><br><span>Three answers, and the third one is where most marks are lost.</span></div><p>Use <strong>TRUE / FALSE / NOT GIVEN</strong> when the questions are about facts in the passage, and <strong>YES / NO / NOT GIVEN</strong> when they are about the writer's views or claims. The thinking is identical; only the labels change.</p><p>TRUE means the passage states this. FALSE means the passage states the <strong>opposite</strong>. NOT GIVEN means the passage does not say either way. The trap is treating <em>&ldquo;I don't think that's right&rdquo;</em> or <em>&ldquo;the passage doesn't mention it&rdquo;</em> as FALSE. If the passage is silent, the answer is NOT GIVEN, however obvious the statement seems.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Answer only from the passage. Your own knowledge of the topic is not evidence.</li><li>FALSE requires contradiction; NOT GIVEN requires absence. Decide which one you can actually point to.</li><li>Watch qualifiers: <em>all / some</em>, <em>always / often</em>, <em>must / may</em>, <em>the first / one of the first</em>.</li><li>These questions run in passage order, so the answer to Q5 sits between Q4's and Q6's.</li><li>Write the word in full — <em>TRUE</em>, not <em>T</em> — unless the instructions allow letters.</li><li>Do not leave one blank; a considered guess between two options is a one-in-two chance.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Passage: <em>&ldquo;Rice yields rose in three of the five counties surveyed.&rdquo;</em><br>Statement A: <em>Yields rose in every county surveyed.</em> &rarr; <strong>FALSE</strong> (the passage contradicts it — three, not five).<br>Statement B: <em>Yields rose because of better seed.</em> &rarr; <strong>NOT GIVEN</strong> (the passage gives no reason at all).</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>There are usually five to eight of these in a paper. Candidates who default to FALSE whenever they cannot find something typically lose three or four marks here — most of a half band, from one habit.</p></div>",
  "Matching Headings, Completion & Multiple Choice": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Matching Headings, Completion & Multiple Choice</strong><br><span>Three common types, three completely different reading speeds.</span></div><p><strong>Matching headings</strong> asks for the main idea of a paragraph, not a detail inside it. There are always more headings than paragraphs, so some are never used. Read the paragraph's first and last sentences, decide what it is <em>about</em> in your own words, then find the heading that says that.</p><p><strong>Completion</strong> (sentence, summary, table, note) needs words taken <strong>exactly from the passage</strong> — do not change the form and do not substitute a synonym. <strong>Multiple choice</strong> gives you paraphrased options, so the right one rarely repeats the passage's words.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Matching headings: do the paragraphs you are sure about first, then eliminate.</li><li>A heading that matches one striking sentence is usually the distractor for that paragraph.</li><li>Completion answers must fit grammatically as well as in meaning — check the sentence still reads correctly.</li><li>Never exceed the word limit in completion tasks, and copy spelling letter for letter.</li><li>Multiple choice: cross out the two clearly wrong options before choosing between the last two.</li><li>Matching headings does <em>not</em> follow passage order; completion and multiple choice usually do.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A paragraph opens <em>&ldquo;Cassava spread quickly after 1900&hellip;&rdquo;</em> and then spends five sentences on how farmers store it. Heading <em>&ldquo;The arrival of a new crop&rdquo;</em> tempts you because of that first line, but the paragraph is really about <em>&ldquo;Methods of preserving the harvest&rdquo;</em>. Main idea, not first line.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>These three types make up most of the paper. Handling each with the right technique — gist for headings, precision for completion, elimination for multiple choice — is worth more than reading faster.</p></div>",
  "Task 1 Academic: Describing Graphs, Charts & Processes": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Task 1 Academic: Describing Graphs, Charts & Processes</strong><br><span>Report what the data shows. Never explain why.</span></div><p>Academic Task 1 gives you a graph, bar chart, pie chart, table, map or process diagram and asks you to <em>summarise the information and report the main features</em>. You have <strong>20 minutes</strong> and must write at least <strong>150 words</strong>. Opinions, causes and recommendations score nothing here — they are off-task.</p><p>The structure that reliably reaches Band 7 has four parts: <strong>paraphrase</strong> the question in one sentence, write an <strong>overview</strong> of the two or three biggest patterns, then two body paragraphs that group and compare the data. The overview is not optional — its absence caps Task Achievement at Band 5.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Paraphrase the prompt; never copy it word for word, as copied text is not counted.</li><li>The overview states the big picture: highest, lowest, biggest change — with <strong>no numbers</strong>.</li><li>Group logically: by trend, by size, or by period — not one sentence per data point.</li><li>Trend verbs: <em>rose, fell, climbed, dropped, plateaued, fluctuated</em>. Adverbs: <em>sharply, steadily, slightly, dramatically</em>.</li><li>Always include units and time frames, and use the right tense for the years shown.</li><li>For a process, use the <strong>passive voice</strong> and sequencers: <em>first, next, once this is done, finally</em>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Overview sentence for a line graph: <em>&ldquo;Overall, cocoa exports grew steadily across the period, while coffee exports fell after an early peak, so that the two crops had swapped positions by the final year.&rdquo;</em> No figures, no reasons — just the shape of the data.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Task 1 is a quarter of your Writing score for a third of the time. A learned four-part structure means you can plan it in two minutes and protect the forty minutes Task 2 genuinely needs.</p></div>",
  "Task 1 General: Formal & Informal Letters": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Task 1 General: Formal & Informal Letters</strong><br><span>Three bullet points, one register, and no mixing.</span></div><p>General Training Task 1 asks for a letter of at least <strong>150 words</strong> in <strong>20 minutes</strong>. The prompt always gives three bullet points, and <strong>all three must be covered</strong> — missing one caps Task Achievement no matter how good the English is.</p><p>Register is decided by who you are writing to. <strong>Formal</strong>: a stranger or an official — <em>Dear Sir or Madam</em> &hellip; <em>Yours faithfully</em>. <strong>Semi-formal</strong>: someone you know by name in a professional role — <em>Dear Mr Kollie</em> &hellip; <em>Yours sincerely</em>. <strong>Informal</strong>: a friend or family member — <em>Dear Musu</em> &hellip; <em>Best wishes</em>.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Open by stating your purpose in the first sentence: <em>I am writing to&hellip;</em></li><li>Give each bullet point its own paragraph — three bullets, three body paragraphs.</li><li>Never mix registers: no contractions or slang in a formal letter, no <em>Yours faithfully</em> to a friend.</li><li><em>Yours faithfully</em> goes with <em>Dear Sir or Madam</em>; <em>Yours sincerely</em> goes with a name.</li><li>Invent specific details freely — dates, names, amounts. Nothing is fact-checked.</li><li>Close with a clear next step: <em>I look forward to your reply</em> / <em>Write back soon</em>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Prompt: complain to your landlord about a leaking roof, saying what the problem is, how it has affected you, and what you want done. Semi-formal, because you know the landlord's name: <em>&ldquo;Dear Mr Weah, I am writing about the roof of the flat I rent at 14 Broad Street&hellip;&rdquo;</em> Three paragraphs, one per bullet, ending <em>Yours sincerely</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Register and bullet coverage are the two things General Task 1 examiners check first. Both are mechanical, both are free marks, and both are lost by candidates whose English is otherwise strong.</p></div>",
  "Task 2: Essay Structure & Building an Argument": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Task 2: Essay Structure & Building an Argument</strong><br><span>Forty minutes, 250 words, and twice the weight of Task 1.</span></div><p>Task 2 asks for a formal essay responding to a statement or question. Write at least <strong>250 words in 40 minutes</strong>. Because it is worth double, do Task 1 first and get to Task 2 with the full forty minutes intact.</p><p>Read the question type carefully, because it decides the structure: <em>opinion</em> (to what extent do you agree?), <em>discussion</em> (discuss both views and give your opinion), <em>problem and solution</em>, <em>advantages and disadvantages</em>, or a <em>two-part question</em>. Answering an opinion prompt with a both-sides essay is off-task even if it is beautifully written.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Introduction</strong>: paraphrase the question, then state your position in one clear sentence.</li><li><strong>Body paragraph</strong>: one main idea, stated first, then explained, then supported with an example.</li><li>Two developed body paragraphs beat four thin ones — depth is what Band 7 rewards.</li><li><strong>Conclusion</strong>: restate your position in different words; add no new idea.</li><li>Take <strong>5 minutes to plan</strong>. A planned essay with simpler English outscores an unplanned one that drifts.</li><li>Under 250 words is penalised, so learn what 250 of your own words look like on the page.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Question: <em>&ldquo;Some people think university education should be free for everyone. To what extent do you agree?&rdquo;</em> This is an opinion prompt, so take a side in the introduction — <em>&ldquo;I largely agree, for two reasons&hellip;&rdquo;</em> — then give one paragraph per reason. Do not write a neutral both-sides essay: it does not answer the question asked.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Task 2 carries two thirds of the Writing band. Identifying the question type and planning for five minutes is the single highest-value habit in the whole exam.</p></div>",
  "Cohesion, Vocabulary & Common Mistakes": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Cohesion, Vocabulary & Common Mistakes</strong><br><span>Half your Writing score is how it hangs together and what words you choose.</span></div><p>Writing is marked on four criteria worth 25% each: Task Achievement, <strong>Coherence and Cohesion</strong>, <strong>Lexical Resource</strong>, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. Coherence is whether your ideas are in a logical order; cohesion is whether the links between them are clear.</p><p>Good cohesion is mostly paragraphing and referencing, not linking words. One idea per paragraph, stated in the first sentence. Then use <em>this</em>, <em>these</em>, <em>such</em>, <em>the latter</em> to connect sentences without repeating nouns.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Start each body paragraph with a topic sentence that states its one idea.</li><li>Use linkers naturally. A paragraph beginning <em>Firstly, Moreover, In addition, Furthermore</em> in four consecutive sentences is marked <em>down</em>, not up.</li><li>Avoid memorised openers such as <em>&ldquo;In this modern era of globalisation&hellip;&rdquo;</em> — examiners discount learned phrases.</li><li>Do not use contractions (<em>don't</em>, <em>it's</em>) or informal words (<em>kids</em>, <em>a lot of</em>) in an essay.</li><li>Keep the introduction to two sentences; long introductions eat the words your argument needs.</li><li>Write under the word count and you are penalised automatically — count once, early, then estimate.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Weak: <em>&ldquo;Firstly, education is important. Moreover, education is good for the economy. In addition, education helps people.&rdquo;</em> Three linkers, one idea, no development.<br>Better: <em>&ldquo;Free tuition widens access to skilled work. When cost no longer decides who studies, employers recruit from the whole population rather than the part that could afford fees — which is why countries that removed tuition saw graduate numbers rise fastest among low-income families.&rdquo;</em></div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Candidates often stall at Band 6 with accurate grammar and a wide vocabulary because their paragraphs have no single controlling idea. Fixing structure lifts two of the four criteria at once.</p></div>",
  "Part 1: Introduction & Interview": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Part 1: Introduction & Interview</strong><br><span>Four minutes of easy questions that decide the examiner's first impression.</span></div><p>Part 1 lasts <strong>4 to 5 minutes</strong>. The examiner checks your ID, then asks about familiar topics: where you live, your work or studies, your hometown, food, weather, hobbies, transport. The English is deliberately easy. What is being tested is whether you can speak <em>naturally</em> at length.</p><p>The target length is <strong>two or three sentences</strong> per answer: give the answer, then a reason or a detail. One-word answers force the examiner to ask another question and show no range. Speeches of thirty seconds are also wrong here — save those for Part 2.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Answer, then extend with <em>because</em>, <em>for example</em>, or a short contrast.</li><li>Do not memorise answers. Examiners hear rehearsed speech immediately and it is penalised.</li><li>It is fine to say you don't know much about something — then say why, in English.</li><li>Use the tense the question uses: <em>Did you</em> &rarr; past, <em>Do you</em> &rarr; present.</li><li>Correct yourself briefly if you slip, then keep going; hesitating to find a perfect word hurts fluency.</li><li>Speak to the examiner as a person. Eye contact and normal intonation are part of the impression.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Q: <em>&ldquo;Do you enjoy cooking?&rdquo;</em><br>Weak: <em>&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;</em><br>Good: <em>&ldquo;I do, though mostly at weekends. During the week I get home late from work, so I cook something quick — but on Sunday I'll spend an hour making jollof rice for my family, and I actually find that relaxing.&rdquo;</em></div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Part 1 is the only section where you already know the topics. Practising extended answers to twenty common questions turns the easiest part of the test into your steadiest marks.</p></div>",
  "Part 2: The Long Turn (Cue Card)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Part 2: The Long Turn (Cue Card)</strong><br><span>One minute to prepare, two minutes to talk, alone.</span></div><p>The examiner hands you a card with a topic, three or four bullet points, and a final line beginning <em>&ldquo;and explain&hellip;&rdquo;</em>. You get paper, a pencil and <strong>one minute</strong> to prepare, then you speak for <strong>one to two minutes</strong> without interruption.</p><p>Use the preparation minute for <strong>keywords, not sentences</strong>. Writing full sentences means you will read them aloud, which sounds flat and is marked down. Five or six words — one per bullet, plus two for the <em>explain</em> line — is enough to keep you moving.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Cover every bullet, but spend the most time on the final <em>explain</em> point; it carries the detail.</li><li>Keep talking until the examiner stops you. Stopping early loses fluency marks.</li><li>If you run dry, add detail: when, where, who with, how you felt, what you would change.</li><li>You may invent the whole story. Nothing is checked for truth, and a made-up example you can describe fluently beats a real one you cannot.</li><li>Use past tenses confidently — most cue cards ask about something you did or experienced.</li><li>Do not ask the examiner questions during the long turn; they will not respond.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Card: <em>Describe a skill you learned that has been useful. Say what it was, how you learned it, how you use it, and explain why it has been useful.</em><br>Notes made in the minute: <em>tailoring &mdash; aunt, 2019 &mdash; school fees &mdash; independence</em>. Six words, two minutes of speech.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Part 2 is the only time you speak uninterrupted, so it is where the examiner hears your real range. Candidates who fill the full two minutes almost always score higher than those who stop at forty seconds.</p></div>",
  "Part 3: Discussion, Fluency & Pronunciation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Part 3: Discussion, Fluency & Pronunciation</strong><br><span>Abstract questions, longer answers, and the marks that separate 6 from 7.</span></div><p>Part 3 runs <strong>4 to 5 minutes</strong> and takes the Part 2 topic into abstract territory: society, comparisons, causes, the future. If Part 2 was about a skill you learned, Part 3 might ask whether schools should teach practical skills, or how job training has changed in your country.</p><p>Answers should be longer and more reasoned than in Part 1 — roughly <strong>four to six sentences</strong>. A reliable shape is <em>opinion &rarr; reason &rarr; example &rarr; contrast or qualification</em>. This is also where you can show conditional and hypothetical grammar, which the examiner is listening for.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Give an opinion even when you have never thought about the topic; the exam tests English, not expertise.</li><li>Use hedging to sound natural: <em>it depends on</em>, <em>I'd say</em>, <em>generally speaking</em>, <em>that said</em>.</li><li>Ask for clarification if you need it — <em>&ldquo;Do you mean in schools specifically?&rdquo;</em> — it is not a penalty.</li><li><strong>Fluency</strong> means keeping going, not speaking fast. Pausing to think is fine; pausing to translate is not.</li><li><strong>Pronunciation</strong> is scored on being understood: word stress, sentence stress and intonation. Your accent is never marked down.</li><li>Vary your sentence length. All-short sentences sound like a list; all-long ones lose the listener.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Q: <em>&ldquo;Do you think practical skills should be taught in secondary school?&rdquo;</em><br><em>&ldquo;I'd say yes, particularly where many students won't go on to university. If a school leaver can repair a phone or keep simple accounts, they can earn straight away. That said, it shouldn't replace academic subjects — the students who do continue still need them.&rdquo;</em> Opinion, reason, example, qualification.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Part 3 is where a candidate who has drilled Parts 1 and 2 can still stall. The fix is not more vocabulary — it is having a habitual shape for an answer so you are never searching for what to say next.</p></div>",
  "Vocabulary for Band 7+: Collocations & Precision": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Vocabulary for Band 7+: Collocations & Precision</strong><br><span>Band 7 vocabulary is precise, not impressive.</span></div><p>Lexical Resource is 25% of your Writing and Speaking bands. The Band 7 descriptor asks for <em>a sufficient range of vocabulary to allow some flexibility and precision</em>, with <em>some less common items</em> and <em>some awareness of style and collocation</em>. Notice what it does not say: long words.</p><p>The fastest gain is <strong>collocation</strong> — which words go together. English says <em>heavy rain</em>, not <em>strong rain</em>; <em>make a decision</em>, not <em>do a decision</em>; <em>a wide range</em>, not <em>a big range</em>. Collocation errors are what makes otherwise correct writing sound non-native.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Learn words in <strong>phrases</strong>, never alone: not <em>research</em>, but <em>conduct research into</em>.</li><li>Precision beats size: <em>a sharp rise</em> is stronger than <em>a very significant increase</em>.</li><li>Build topic families — education, health, environment, work, technology — with ten phrases each.</li><li>Do not swap in a thesaurus word you have never seen in a sentence; wrong usage costs more than repetition.</li><li>Some repetition is fine. Forcing three synonyms for one idea is a classic Band 6 signal.</li><li>Learn the word family: <em>economy, economic, economical, economically</em> — the wrong form is a lexical error.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Weak: <em>&ldquo;The government must do big steps to solve the problem of unemployment which is very big in the country.&rdquo;</em><br>Band 7: <em>&ldquo;The government must take decisive steps to tackle the high unemployment rate.&rdquo;</em> Same idea, five fewer words, three correct collocations — <em>take steps</em>, <em>tackle</em>, <em>unemployment rate</em>.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Candidates plateau at Band 6 by memorising rare words and using them slightly wrongly. Twenty accurate collocations per topic move the score further than two hundred isolated words.</p></div>",
  "Grammar Range: Complex Sentences Without Errors": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Grammar Range: Complex Sentences Without Errors</strong><br><span>Range and accuracy are marked together, so one without the other caps you.</span></div><p>Grammatical Range and Accuracy is another 25%. Band 7 requires <em>a variety of complex structures</em> and <em>frequent error-free sentences</em>. Both halves matter: simple, perfect writing caps at 6, and ambitious writing riddled with errors also caps at 6.</p><p>A <strong>complex sentence</strong> is not a long one. It is a main clause plus at least one subordinate clause. <em>&ldquo;Although fees were reduced, enrolment fell.&rdquo;</em> is complex and only six words long.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Relative clauses: <em>Farmers who adopted the new seed reported higher yields.</em></li><li>Subordinators: <em>although, while, whereas, because, unless, even though</em>.</li><li>Conditionals: <em>If fees were abolished, enrolment would rise.</em> Especially useful in Speaking Part 3.</li><li>The passive, where the doer is unimportant: <em>The rice is dried before it is milled.</em></li><li>Accuracy checklist — articles (<em>a/an/the</em>), subject-verb agreement, plurals, tense consistency, prepositions.</li><li>Leave two minutes at the end of each writing task to check only those five things.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Simple: <em>&ldquo;Many students leave school early. They need money. They start working.&rdquo;</em><br>Complex and accurate: <em>&ldquo;Many students who leave school early do so because they need an income, which means they start working before completing their qualifications.&rdquo;</em> One sentence, a relative clause, a causal clause and a result clause — with nothing to correct.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Examiners count error-free sentences. Writing three structures you control perfectly will always score higher than eight you half-remember.</p></div>",
  "The Full Mock Test: Practice Under Real Exam Conditions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 The Full Mock Test: Practice Under Real Exam Conditions</strong><br><span>Practice only counts when the conditions are honest.</span></div><p>A mock test is not a set of exercises. It is <strong>Listening, Reading and Writing back to back, timed, in one sitting</strong>, with no pauses, no dictionary, and no replaying the audio. Anything gentler tells you what you could score with help, which is not the number on your Test Report Form.</p><p>Do the Speaking separately, recorded. Play it back — hearing your own hesitations and repeated words is uncomfortable and more useful than any feedback you will get from reading about fluency.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Listening: play the recording <strong>once</strong>, at full length, and stop when it stops.</li><li>Reading: set a 60-minute timer and copy answers onto a sheet <em>inside</em> the hour.</li><li>Writing: 60 minutes for both tasks, handwritten if you will sit the paper test.</li><li>No breaks between the three papers. Stamina is part of what you are training.</li><li>Mark it honestly against the answer key — spelling and word limits included.</li><li>Then do <strong>error analysis</strong>: sort every lost mark by question type, not by passage.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A candidate scores 26/40 on Reading. Sorting the 14 lost marks shows nine came from True/False/Not Given and matching headings, and only two from vocabulary. The next fortnight's study is now obvious — and it is not &ldquo;read more&rdquo;.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Mock tests measure two things you cannot practise piecemeal: timing and stamina. Two honest mocks with careful error analysis are worth more than a month of untimed exercises.</p></div>",
  "Your 30-Day Study Plan & Test-Week Checklist": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Your 30-Day Study Plan & Test-Week Checklist</strong><br><span>Thirty days is enough — if the plan targets your weakest skill.</span></div><p>Start by finding your baseline with one full mock, then build the month around the skill it exposed. The overall band is an average, so a month spent lifting a 5.5 to a 6.5 changes your result far more than a month polishing a skill already at 7.</p><p>A workable shape: <strong>Week 1</strong> diagnose and fix fundamentals; <strong>Week 2</strong> drill question types one at a time, untimed; <strong>Week 3</strong> full sections under time; <strong>Week 4</strong> complete mocks with error analysis, then taper.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Daily core: one Listening section, one Reading passage, one writing task, 15 minutes speaking aloud.</li><li>Alternate Task 1 and Task 2 on writing days; do not skip Task 1 because it feels easier.</li><li>Keep a single error log for all four skills and re-read it every Sunday.</li><li>Record your Speaking twice a week and listen back — it is the fastest fluency feedback available.</li><li>Do your last full mock <strong>five days before</strong> the test, not the day before.</li><li>Final 48 hours: review your error log and vocabulary lists only. No new material.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Test-week checklist: confirm the date, time and venue; check your <strong>Speaking</strong> slot separately; put the exact ID from registration with your confirmation email the night before; plan to arrive 30 minutes early; sleep normally for three nights, not just one; eat before you leave, because the first three papers run for nearly three hours without a break.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Most candidates who miss their target band did not run out of ability — they ran out of plan, and spent the month practising whatever felt comfortable rather than whatever was costing them marks.</p></div>",
  "Project: Write a Full Band 7 Task 2 Essay": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Project: Write a Full Band 7 Task 2 Essay</strong><br><span>One essay, written under exam conditions, marked against the real criteria.</span></div><p>This project asks you to produce a complete Task 2 essay of <strong>at least 250 words in 40 minutes</strong>, handwritten if you will sit the paper test, then assess it against the four public band descriptors rather than simply reading it back.</p><p>Choose a question type you find hardest — for most candidates that is <em>discuss both views and give your opinion</em> — because the point of the project is to expose a weakness while there is still time to fix it.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Plan for <strong>5 minutes</strong>: question type, your position, one idea per body paragraph, one example each.</li><li>Write for <strong>32 minutes</strong> without stopping to look anything up.</li><li>Check for <strong>3 minutes</strong>: articles, subject-verb agreement, plurals, tense, word count.</li><li>Then score yourself on all four criteria — Task Response, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, Grammar.</li><li>Count your error-free sentences. Band 7 needs them to be <em>frequent</em>, not occasional.</li><li>Rewrite only the weakest paragraph, not the whole essay, and compare the two versions.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Suggested question: <em>&ldquo;Some people believe governments should invest in public transport rather than roads. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.&rdquo;</em> Keep the plan, the timed draft and your marked-up copy together — they show exactly which criterion is holding your band down.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Keep this essay. Repeating the same project in week four with a different question, and comparing the two marked copies, is the clearest evidence you will get that your writing has actually moved.</p></div>"
};
})();
